var e={"09-05":{saint:`Holy Prophet Zacharias & Righteous Elizabeth, Parents of the Forerunner`,oca_primary:!0,source_file:`09-05A.pdf`,rank:`polyeleos`,fekula_section:`2E`,has_great_doxology:!0,has_polyeleos:!0,has_litya:!0,has_paroemias:!0,magnificat_sung:!0,matins_format:`god_is_the_lord`,matins_gospel:`Luke 1:5-25 (§2)`,aposticha_source:`menaion`,magnification:`We magnify you, O holy and righteous Zacharias and Elizabeth, and we honor your holy memory; for ye entreat Christ our God on our behalf.`,magnification_selected_psalm:`Blessed be the Lord God of Israel; for He hath visited and wrought redemption for His people.`,note:"RANK IS PRINTED, NOT INFERRED. ODS 3rd ed. vol. III (Services of the Menaion), pp. 14-16, entry `ods3-0905-wkd`, states 'A polyeleos-rank service on Monday through Saturday'. Per encoding_rule_v2.md §1.1 step 0 a printed rank is decisive and short-circuits the waterfall; steps 1-6 were NOT run. Vol. III supersedes ODS 2nd ed. ch. V for this date (§1 priority 1). SOURCE FILE IS 09-05A.pdf, not 09-05.pdf. 09-05.pdf prints the Zacharias-only service (6 stichera, no paroemias, no Polyeleos, no Great Doxology — six_stichera §2C on its own); 09-05A.pdf prints the combined Zacharias + Elizabeth polyeleos service that vol. III prescribes and that OCA's primary commemoration names. The two files are the same commemoration at two service levels, NOT two commemorations, so this is a single entry rather than an array — an array would emit Zacharias twice. KONTAKION ROUTING CORROBORATED INDEPENDENTLY: the PDF prints Zacharias' kontakion after Ode III and Elizabeth's after Ode VI, which §5 routes to the 1st/6th and 3rd/9th Hours respectively. Vol. III p.16 states exactly that routing in words ('First Hour and Sixth Hour: kontakion of St. Zacharias. Third Hour and Ninth Hour: kontakion of St. Elizabeth'). Two sources agreeing without being derived from each other. LIC: 8 slots from 6 unique texts — 4 of Zacharias (Tone IV, first repeated) and 4 of Elizabeth (Tone I, first repeated). Not uniform doubling (8/6 is not integral), so positional repeatIndex markers are used per §6b. MATINS APOSTICHA: ABSENT by rubric, not unencoded — the Praises are followed directly by the Great Doxology (PDF p.17), which displaces the Matins Aposticha. TWO DIVERGENCES FROM ODS vol. III, both flagged rather than reconciled: (1) vol. III appoints the Litiya 'Now & ever' as the Aposticha theotokion OF THE RESURRECTION, Tone VI, and the Vespers Aposticha 'Now & ever' likewise of the resurrection, Tone II — i.e. routed to the Octoechos; the St. Sergius PDF instead prints proper Menaion theotokia in those slots (both captured here). (2) vol. III notes the Litiya 'is usually not an element of a polyeleos-rank commemoration; however, if the rector so direct, it may be performed, but without the blessing of the loaves' — the PDF prints the Litiya stichera unconditionally. has_litya is true on the PDF's authority; the conditional is a rubrical note, not a data state. OCA CHECK — no override applied. OCA's Troparion Tone 2 is the same hymn as the PDF's 'Troparion of the righteous ones, in Tone II' in contemporary register; per §11 #17 the thou/thy PDF text governs. OCA's Kontakion Tone 3 is the same as the PDF's Zacharias kontakion. OCA additionally lists a second, Zacharias-specific troparion in Tone 4 ('Robed in the vestments of the priesthood...') which the St. Sergius PDF does NOT print; recorded here as an OCA-only text, not encoded, since the PDF's proper stands and §2.1 step 5 permits an OCA override only where the daily PDF lacks a proper. IKOS — BOTH CAPTURED, NO SCHEMA CHANGE. The PDF prints TWO ikoi: Zacharias' after Ode III, and a joint Zacharias-and-Elizabeth ikos after Ode VI ('As God once inscribed His commandments upon two tablets of stone...'). `ikos_ode3` and `ikos_ode6` were already in KNOWN_FIELDS and already in use in pentecostarion.js, so capturing both required no vocabulary change. Placement is driven by the renderer: menaion-browser.jsx hardcodes the label 'Ikos (after Ode VI)' for `entry.ikos`, so the Ode VI ikos is stored there to keep that label truthful, and Zacharias' Ode III ikos is stored in `ikos_ode3`. KNOWN DISPLAY GAP: the Menaion browser does not yet render ikos_ode3 (it is a Pentecostarion-browser field today), so the Ode III ikos is in the data but not on screen. Recorded rather than worked around. SCOPE: full canon odes, sessional hymns and Praises stichera are out of scope at polyeleos rank per §6 and are present in the PDF but deliberately not captured.",feast_e:`Hebrews 6:13-20 (§314)`,feast_g:`Matthew 23:29-39 (§96)`,prokeimenon_tone:7,prokeimenon_text:`The righteous man shall be glad in the Lord, * and shall hope in Him.`,prokeimenon_stichos:`Hearken, O God, unto my prayer, when I make supplication unto Thee.`,alleluia_tone:7,alleluia_verse:`A light hath dawned for the righteous man, and gladness for the upright of heart.`,alleluia_stichos:`NOT IN PDF — the PDF prints the Alleluia verse without a second stichos.`,communion_verse:`Rejoice in the Lord, O ye righteous; praise is meet for the upright.`,matins_prokeimenon:`In the saints that are in His earth hath the Lord been wondrous; * He hath wrought all His desires in them. (Tone IV) Verse: Wondrous is God in His saints, the God of Israel.`,paroemia_1:`Genesis — the Lord appears to Abraham at the oak of Mamre and promises Sarah a son (Genesis 18:1-14)`,paroemia_2:`Judges — the angel announces the birth of Samson to the barren wife of Manoah (Judges 13:2-20)`,paroemia_3:`First Kings (1 Samuel) — Hannah prays at Shiloh and conceives Samuel (First Kings 1:9-20)`,stichera_lord_i_call_count:8,stichera_lord_i_call:[{tone:4,spec_mel:`Thou hast granted a sign`,text:`With the oil of anointing and holy vesture upon thyself, * O ever-memorable Zacharias, * thou didst minister unto God like an angel, * mediating, O blessed one, * between the Creator and His creation, * and manifestly receiving the words of the divine Spirit. * Wherefore, we call thee blessed, * and celebrate today thy holy festival, ** glorifying the Savior.`},{repeatIndex:0},{tone:4,spec_mel:`Thou hast granted a sign`,text:`O divinely inspired Zacharias, * thou didst behold born of a maiden * a Babe Who is co-beginningless with the Father; * and unto thine own child thou dost prophecy manifestly beforehand: * “A prophet shalt thou truly be, * preparing His ways!” * With him we call thee blessed * and in a sacred manner we celebrate thine honored festival, ** O most noetically rich God-bearer.`},{tone:4,spec_mel:`Thou hast granted a sign`,text:`Thou wast the living and animate temple * of the divine Spirit; * and, ministering unto God * with a pure heart, O glorious one, * in the midst of the temple thou wast unjustly slain, * finishing thy godly course in martyrdom, O right-glorious one. * Wherefore, with thine own blood * thou hast entered into the temple of heaven, ** entreating cleansing for our souls.`},{tone:1,spec_mel:`Joy of the ranks of heaven`,text:`When thou didst call the Virgin who greeted thee * the Mother of thy Lord, * thou didst behold in her virginal womb * Him, incarnate, Whose goings forth are from before time began. * Pray with her, O righteous Elizabeth, ** that He save our souls.`},{repeatIndex:4},{tone:1,spec_mel:`Joy of the ranks of heaven`,text:`“Blessed is the Fruit of the Virgin’s womb, * Who filleth all things!”, * thou didst exclaim, O most blessed Elizabeth, * moved thus to speak by the Spirit of God. * Wherefore, honoring thee in hymns, ** we magnify God Who is worshiped in Trinity.`},{tone:1,spec_mel:`Joy of the ranks of heaven`,text:`That which was said by the Lord * became reality in the Virgin, * for she gave birth to Jesus Who saveth His people from their sins. * “He shall be the fulfillment of those things which were said to her,” * thou didst exclaim, inspired by the Holy Spirit, O Elizabeth. ** Wherefore, we crown thee with wreaths of praise, as is meet.`}],stichera_glory:{tone:8,text:`Truly arrayed in the vestments of the priesthood of the law, thou didst serve according to the order of Aaron; and, standing in the temple, thou didst clearly behold the countenance of the angel, O most blessed one. Wherefore, hymning thy repose as is meet, O Zacharias, we all praise with songs thee who, in thine old age, begat the glorious John. Entreat thou the merciful God for us, that we be saved.`},lic_theotokion:{tone:8,text:`In His love for mankind, the King of heaven appeared on earth * and dwelt among men; * for He Who received flesh from the pure Virgin * and cameth forth from her having received human nature, * is the only Son of God, * twofold in nature but not Hypostasis. * Therefore, proclaiming Him to be truly perfect God and perfect man, * we confess Christ our God. * Him do thou beseech, O unwedded Mother, ** that our souls find mercy!`},litya_stichera:[{tone:6,text:`Seeing Jesus, John cried aloud: “Behold the Lamb of God!” fulfilling the prophecy of Isaiah. Thy son was the last of the prophets, O Elizabeth. Wherefore, the people call thee blessed, as is meet.`},{tone:6,text:`Jesus, Who is the Lord of lords and Master of masters, bowed His head beneath the right hand of thy son. Great is thy glory, O right-laudable Elizabeth!`},{tone:6,text:`The voice of one crying in the wilderness denounced the boastful Pharisees, saying unto them: “O generation of vipers, bring forth fruits worthy of repentance!” And he worshiped the Master. Of such a son art thou the mother, O holy Elizabeth!`}],litya_glory:{tone:6,text:`Keeping the commandments of God, thou didst bring forth the fruit of living faith which dwelt within thee, O divinely wise Elizabeth. With what worthy hymns, with what worthy praises can we, the faithful, crown thee?`},litya_both_now:{tone:6,text:`Christ the Lord, my Creator and Redeemer, * Who came forth from thy womb, O most pure one, * and clothed Himself in my nature, * hath freed Adam from the primal curse. * Wherefore, like the angel * we unceasingly cry out to thee, * O all-pure one, * who art truly the Mother of God and Virgin: * Rejoice!, O Sovereign Lady, ** the intercession, protection and salvation of our souls!`},stichera_aposticha:[{tone:1,text:`Thou art worthy of many praises, O divinely wise Elizabeth, who art called righteous by the word of God, thou didst walk blamelessly in the commandments of the Lord, moved so by the mercy of God.`},{tone:1,verse:`Precious in the sight of the Lord * is the death of His saints.`,text:`Walking in the commandments of the Lord, O abundantly glorious Elizabeth, thou didst have unfeigned love for God and unfeigned love for thy neighbor. And, lo! the fruit of thy faith was made reality. Wherefore, holding festival, we call thee blessed.`},{tone:1,verse:`Blessed is the man that feareth the Lord; * in His commandments shall he greatly delight.`,text:`A most glorious fruit of the womb was given by the most compassionate God to thee, a woman barren and past childbearing: the preacher of repentance. Wherefore, calling thee blessed, we magnify the almighty mercy of God.`}],aposticha_glory:{tone:2,text:`As a pure priest thou didst enter into the holy of holies and, clad in sacred vesture, blamelessly ministered unto God, observing the law like Aaron and leading the tribes of Israel like Moses, in the pure signaling of the little bells. Wherefore, thou wast slain. But thy righteous blood hath become for us a salvific healing, like fragrant myrrh opening the ears of the deaf leading them to the way of everlasting life. O thrice blessed Zacharias, father of John the Baptist and husband of Elizabeth: pray thou earnestly on behalf of our souls.`},aposticha_both_now:{tone:2,text:`O new wonder greater than all the wonders of old! * For who hath ever known a mother to give birth without having known a man, * and to bear on her arm Him Who sustaineth all creation? * Yet it was the will of God to be born. * O most pure one, who carried Him as an infant in Thine embrace * and before Whom thou hast a mother’s boldness: * cease not to pray on behalf of those who honor thee, ** that He have compassion and save our souls.`},troparion:{tone:2,text:`Celebrating the memory of Thy righteous ones, * Zacharias and Elizabeth, O Lord, * through them do we entreat Thee: ** Save Thou our souls!`},troparion_bothnow:{tone:2,text:`All of thy most glorious mysteries are beyond comprehension, * O Theotokos; * for, thy purity sealed and thy virginity intact, * thou art known to be a true Mother, having given birth unto God. ** Him do thou entreat, that our souls be saved.`},kontakion_ode3:{tone:3,spec_mel:`Today the Virgin`,text:`Today Zacharias, the prophet and high priest of the Most High, * the father of the Forerunner, * setteth the table of his memorial, * feeding the faithful; * for he hath distributed the food of righteousness unto all. * Wherefore, he hath reposed * as a divine initiate ** of the mysteries of the grace of God.`},kontakion_ode6:{tone:4,text:`Like the full moon, * thou didst receive the light of righteousness from the Messiah, * the noetic Sun, * O Elizabeth beloved of God, * and with Zacharias didst walk in all the commandments of the Lord. * Wherefore, blessing thee with worthy hymns, * we magnify the Lord, ** the most compassionate Light, Who illumineth all.`},ikos_ode3:`The high priest of the law was bound to silence by the voice of an angel, for within the barren and chaste Elizabeth, the angel of the coming of Christ, the prophet and initiate of the mysteries was to come forth. By his nativity, the grace, deliverance, universal reconciliation and renewal of our nature was to be revealed. By Him Who giveth fruit from the barren womb, the Son Who hath appeared from the Virgin, the divine initiate of the mysteries of the grace of God and his preaching of the Lamb was to be made manifest.`,ikos:`As God once inscribed His commandments upon two tablets of stone, so hath He illumined you with His grace, O blessed Zacharias and Elizabeth; for ye walked blamelessly in His commandments. Therefore, worthily praising you, we magnify the Lord of all, the most compassionate Light Who illumineth all.`,gospel_sticheron:{tone:1,text:`That which was said by the Lord became reality in the Virgin, for she gave birth to Jesus Who saveth His people from their sins. “He shall be the fulfillment of those things which were said to her,” thou didst exclaim, inspired by the Holy Spirit, O Elizabeth. Wherefore, we crown thee with wreaths of praise, as is meet.`},matins_exapostilarion_feast:{spec_mel:`O Light immutable`,text:`At first, thou wast struck mute in the holy of holies, by the voice of the angel, for the springing forth from thee of the voice of one crying in the wilderness; and now thou art silent for his sake, slain, O Zacharias, before the holy of holies.`},exapostilarion:`Thou wast the glory of barren mothers, the model of virtues for sinners, O blessed Elizabeth, keeping the commandments of the Lord. We glorify the goodness of God which hath been made manifest in thee.`,matins_exapostilarion_theotokion:{text:`Having shone forth eternally, Light from Light, before the morning star, O Word, Thou didst come forth as Light, immutably, under time, from Thy Mother, illumining the ends of the world with radiant effulgence, as Thou art the Light of the world.`},beatitudes_source:`menaion`,beatitudes_troparia:[{text:`When thou didst offer the incense of the covenant, O high priest, then didst thou receive the prophetic proclamation of the Forerunner.`,source:`Ode III`,label:`Ode III, 1`,note:`(Twice) per PDF`},{text:`The priesthood anointed thee with oil to serve as a priest, like Aaron. Wherefore, thou wast deemed worthy to behold the angel.`,source:`Ode III`,label:`Ode III, 2`},{text:`Zacharias begat John, the dawn which announced to the whole world Thee, the noetic Sun, O Savior.`,source:`Ode III`,label:`Ode III, 3`},{text:`When John was in thy womb, O holy Elizabeth, seeing His Savior he, the babe, did leap. Blessed is thy womb which made manifest the babe who recognized Jesus as his salvation.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI, 1`},{text:`Strengthened by the Holy Spirit against the will of others, O most blessed Elizabeth, thou didst name thy son John. Let everyone praise thee, the instrument of the Holy Spirit.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI, 2`},{text:`O God, Thou didst show forth Elizabeth’s conceiving as an image of Thine own inconceivable birthgiving from the Virgin. Glory to Thy mighty wisdom!`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI, 3`},{text:`When he was born of Rachel, Benjamin caused his own mother’s death; but the Word Who was born of thee is the true life of all. Him do thou beseech, that we who place our trust in Him may have everlasting life.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI, Theotokion`}]},"09-06":{saint:`Commemoration of the Miracle of the Archangel Michael at Colossae (Chonae)`,oca_primary:!0,source_file:`09-06.pdf`,rank:`six_stichera`,fekula_section:`2C`,has_great_doxology:!1,has_polyeleos:!1,has_litya:!1,has_paroemias:!1,magnificat_sung:!1,matins_format:`god_is_the_lord`,aposticha_source:`octoechos`,note:"Rank per encoding_rule_v2.md §1.1 waterfall, run in full because ODS 3rd ed. vol. III does NOT cover 6 September (no `ods3-0906` entry among the 77) — so §1.1 step 0 does not apply. Not a Great Feast; no Small Vespers in 09-06.pdf (not Vigil); no Polyeleos or Magnification appointed at Matins (not Polyeleos); no Great Doxology appointed (not Doxology); 6 stichera at Lord I Have Cried for a single commemoration, all 'of the holy archangel' → Six-Stichera §2C. NOT A DOUBLE: the Martyr Eudoxius is co-commemorated and the PDF gives him a full canon at Compline, but he has NO stichera at Lord I Have Cried, so the 6 stichera are not a 3+3 split and §1.1's Simple-rank double test does not fire. IN 2026 THIS DATE FALLS ON A SUNDAY — the 14th Sunday after Pentecost, Tone 5. The rank recorded here is the saint's own, as printed in the Menaion, per §1.1's governing principle that rank does not depend on what weekday the fixed date happens to fall on. Sunday layering is the assembler's job, not this entry's. Note that OCA lists ONLY the Sunday readings for 6 September 2026 (Matins Gospel Mark 16:9-20; Liturgy 2 Cor 1:21-2:4 and Matt 22:1-14); the Hebrews 2:2-10 / Luke 10:16-21 propers recorded below are the archangel's own weekday-service propers as printed in the PDF, and on a Sunday they would be combined or displaced per Fekula ch. 1. Both are recorded; neither is silently preferred. APOSTICHA: 'On the Aposticha, the Stichera from the Oktoechos' — §2C, Octoechos governs, so no stichera_aposticha array. The PDF prints ONE combined doxasticon labelled 'Glory ..., Both now ..., the composition of John the Monk, in Tone VIII' serving both slots; it is stored once in aposticha_glory and aposticha_both_now is therefore omitted rather than duplicated. BEATITUDES: the PDF appoints 8 troparia drawn entirely from the archangel's own canon (four each from Odes III and VI). This is richer than the usual §2C pattern of 4 Octoechos + 4 from Ode III (cf. 07-01) and is encoded as printed, not normalized. OCR ARTIFACT, NOT A SOURCE DEFECT: the extracted Gospel heading reads 'ST. LUKE (1O:16-21)' with a capital letter O in place of the zero. This is an extraction artifact of the glyph, not an error in the printed book, and is corrected to Luke 10:16-21 here. Distinguished from the §1.3 errata class, which are defects in the printed source itself. SCOPE: full canon odes (both the archangel's at Matins and Eudoxius' at Compline), sessional hymns and Praises stichera are out of scope at this rank per §6 and are present in the PDF but deliberately not captured.",feast_e:`Hebrews 2:2-10`,feast_g:`Luke 10:16-21`,prokeimenon_tone:4,prokeimenon_text:`He maketh His angels spirits, * and His ministers a flame of fire.`,prokeimenon_stichos:`Bless the Lord, O my soul; O Lord my God, Thou hast been magnified exceedingly.`,alleluia_tone:5,alleluia_verse:`Praise Him, all ye His angels; praise Him, all ye His hosts.`,alleluia_stichos:`For He spake, and they came to be; He commanded, and they were created.`,communion_verse:`He maketh His angels spirits, and His ministers a flame of fire.`,stichera_lord_i_call_count:6,stichera_lord_i_call:[{tone:4,spec_mel:`As one valiant among the martyrs`,text:`O Michael, supreme commander, * shown forth as a most radiant intercessor before the three-Sunned Godhead, * thou dost cry out, rejoicing with the heavenly hosts: * Holy art Thou, O Father! * Holy art Thou, O co-beginningless Son! * Holy art Thou also, O Holy Spirit! * One glory, * one Kingdom and nature, ** one Godhead and power!`},{tone:4,spec_mel:`As one valiant among the martyrs`,text:`Thine aspect is fiery, * and thy beauty wondrous, * O Michael, first among angels, * for in thine immaterial nature * thou dost traverse the ends of the earth, * fulfilling the commandments of the Creator of all, * known in the power of thy might, * making thy church a wellspring of healings, ** revered in thy holy name.`},{tone:4,spec_mel:`As one valiant among the martyrs`,text:`O Thou Who, as it is written, * doth make Thine angels spirits * and Thy ministers a flame of fire: * amid the ranks of Thine archangels, O Lord, * Thou hast shown forth as preeminent * Michael, the supreme commander, * who doth obey Thy commands, O Word, * and with fear doth utter ** the thrice-holy hymn unto Thy glory.`}],stichera_glory:{tone:6,text:`Rejoice with us, all ye angelic legions, for the great supreme commander, your superior and our intercessor, appearing most wondrously this day in his honored temple, doth sanctify it. Wherefore, chanting as is meet, let us cry aloud to him: Cover us with the shelter of thy wings, O Michael, thou greatest of the archangels!`},lic_theotokion:{tone:6,text:`Rejoice with us, all ye choirs of virgins, for our intercessor, mediator, protection and great refuge doth today on her divine and honored feast comfort the sorrowful. Wherefore, hymning her at length as is meet, let us cry aloud: Protect us with thy divine intercession, O most pure Lady Theotokos!`},aposticha_glory:{tone:8,text:`O supreme commander, as general, champion and chief of the angels, from all want and grief, from afflictions and grievous sins do thou free those who hymn and beseech thee with faith, O glorious one, for thou art manifestly immaterial, beholding the Immaterial One, illumined with the unapproachable light of the Master’s glory. For in His love for mankind He took flesh from the Virgin for our sake, wishing to save the human race.`},troparion:{tone:4,text:`O supreme commander of the heavenly hosts, * we entreat thee unworthy though we be, * that by thy prayers, thou wilt encompass us * with the protection of the wings of thine immaterial glory * preserving us who fall down before thee and earnestly cry aloud: * deliver us from all misfortunes, ** for thou art the supreme commander of the hosts on high.`},kontakion_ode6:{tone:2,text:`O chief commander of God, minister of divine glory, * captain of the angels and instructor of all mankind: * beg thou great mercy and that which is profitable for us, * for thou art the supreme commander ** of the bodiless hosts.`},ikos:`In Thy Scriptures, O immortal Lover of mankind, Thou didst say that a multitude of angels rejoiceth in heaven over one man that repenteth. Wherefore, O Sinless One, Who alone knowest the hearts of men, amid our transgressions we ever make bold to beseech Thee to have compassion and to send down compunction upon us, unworthy though we be, granting us forgiveness, O Master, in that Thou art compassionate; for the supreme commander of the bodiless hosts doth entreat Thee on behalf of us all.`,exapostilarion:`As of old thou didst subdue the raging flow and thundering of the river, O glorious supreme commander, so now destroy the pride of the heathen and the raging of the demons, that we may fittingly honor thee as is meet.`,matins_exapostilarion_theotokion:{text:`The ranks of the bodiless hosts honor thy birthgiving, for thou alone hast filled mortals with joy. Wherefore, we, the faithful, glorify thee the all-immaculate one, singing the praises of thy glory in hymns, for thou hast shone forth light upon those who are in darkness, like the dawn which shineth forth in the morning.`},beatitudes_source:`menaion`,beatitudes_troparia:[{text:`With divine might dost thou encircle all the earth, O all-praised chief among the angels, rescuing us that call upon thy divine name from the cruel enemy.`,source:`Ode III`,label:`Ode III, 1`,note:`(Twice) per PDF`},{text:`O supreme commander of God, of aspect most divine, thou hast been a divine herald, an undaunted intercessor for the faithful, a guide to those who are astray and an instructor.`,source:`Ode III`,label:`Ode III, 2`},{text:`Thou wast a most pure mirror of the splendor of God, radiantly reflecting the manifestation of the honored Spirit, O right wondrous Michael, first among the angels.`,source:`Ode III`,label:`Ode III, 3`},{text:`The multitude of the faithful rejoiceth, praising thee, O Michael, and it doth glorify the most holy Word Who in His goodness hath united men and angels.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI, 1`},{text:`Thou didst save the armies of Israel, revealing thyself and transmitting the commands of God, O supreme commander; and thou didst cast down the enemy and didst utterly destroy them.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI, 2`},{text:`Seized with fear and reverence, Joshua, son of Nun, bowed down when he beheld, thee, O chief of the angels, asking thine honored and holy name.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI, 3`},{text:`Blessed are the people that ever bless thee, O blessed one, who hast given birth unto the blessed God, Who, in His tender compassion, hath deified mortal man by an ineffable union.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI, Theotokion`}]},"09-07":{saint:`Forefeast of the Nativity of the Most Holy Theotokos; Commemoration of the Holy Martyr Sozon`,oca_primary:!0,source_file:`09-07.pdf`,rank:`six_stichera`,fekula_section:`2G1`,fekula_section_override:`2G1`,has_great_doxology:!1,has_polyeleos:!1,has_litya:!1,has_paroemias:!1,magnificat_sung:!1,matins_format:`god_is_the_lord`,aposticha_source:`menaion`,note:"RANK PER encoding_rule_v2.md §1.1 WATERFALL, RUN IN FULL because ODS 3rd ed. vol. III does not cover 7 September (no `ods3-0907` entry), so §1.1 step 0 (printed rank) does not apply. Step 1 — not a Great Feast: 7 September is the FOREfeast of the Nativity of the Theotokos, and a forefeast is not itself a member of the closed enumerated list of Twelve Great Feasts (8 September is). Step 2 — not Vigil: 09-07.pdf prints no Small Vespers. Step 3 — not Polyeleos: Matins appoints neither Polyeleos nor Magnification nor a Matins Gospel; the two Sessional Hymns follow ordinary chantings of the Psalter. Step 4 — not Doxology: no Great Doxology is appointed, and the PDF prints a Matins Aposticha, which is positive rather than merely negative evidence, since the Great Doxology displaces the Matins Aposticha wherever it is sung (cf. the 09-05 entry, where the reverse holds). Step 5 — Six-Stichera: 'On “Lord, I have cried ...,” 6 Stichera: 3 of the forefeast, in Tone I ... And 3 Stichera of the holy martyr, in Tone IV'. A 3-forefeast + 3-saint split is the forefeast-with-saint pattern, so fekula_section and fekula_section_override are both 2G1 rather than the plain-saint 2C. APOSTICHA SOURCE IS MENAION, printed verbatim: 'On the Aposticha, these Stichera of the forefeast, in Tone VIII'. The three stichera are therefore encoded with their psalm verses on the stichera they precede, exactly as the PDF lays them out (the first sticheron carries no verse; verses precede stichera 2 and 3). KONTAKION ROUTING (§5): the PDF prints the Martyr Sozon's kontakion (Tone II, Spec. Mel. “The steadfast ...”) after ODE III, and the forefeast kontakion (Tone III, Spec. Mel. “Today the Virgin ...”) after ODE VI. Stored as kontakion_ode3 = Sozon → 1st & 6th Hours, kontakion_ode6 = forefeast → 3rd & 9th Hours. Both are reprinted together in the AT LITURGY block in the same order, which corroborates the pairing without being a second independent witness (it is the same book). TWO IKOI, BOTH CAPTURED. Sozon's ikos is printed immediately after his Ode III kontakion and is stored in `ikos_ode3`; the forefeast ikos is printed after the Ode VI kontakion and is stored in `ikos`, because menaion-browser.jsx hardcodes the label 'Ikos (after Ode VI)' for `entry.ikos` and storing it there keeps that label truthful. KNOWN DISPLAY GAP CARRIED OVER FROM 09-05: menaion-browser.jsx does not yet render ikos_ode3, so Sozon's ikos is in the data but not on screen. COMBINED GLORY/BOTH-NOW, TWICE. At Lord I Have Cried the PDF prints ONE hymn under the single heading 'Glory ..., Both now ..., of the forefeast, in Tone IV', and at the Aposticha likewise ONE hymn under 'Glory ..., Both now ..., the composition of Germanus, in Tone IV'. Each is stored once — in stichera_glory and aposticha_glory respectively — and the Both-now partner fields are OMITTED rather than duplicated, following the 09-06 precedent. Duplicating would misrepresent one hymn as two. FRIDAY-EVENING VARIANT NOT ENCODED: the PDF adds 'But if it be Friday evening: Glory ..., the above Doxasticon of the Forefeast; Both now ..., the Dogmaticon of the current tone.' This is a weekday-conditional Octoechos substitution with no proper text of its own, so there is nothing to store; it is recorded here so the rubric is not lost. TROPARION ORDER — PRINTED ORDER PRESERVED, MATINS INVERSION FLAGGED. At Vespers the PDF prints 'Troparion of the holy martyr, in Tone IV' first, then 'Glory ..., Both now ..., Troparion of the forefeast, in Tone IV'; `troparion` therefore holds Sozon's and `troparion_second` the forefeast's. At 'God is the Lord' the order INVERTS: the forefeast troparion (Twice), Glory — the martyr's, Both now — the forefeast (Once). The inversion is recorded here rather than silently normalized in either direction, since the Hours take the forefeast troparion first. NOTE also that menaion-browser.jsx does not currently render `troparion_second`. EXAPOSTILARION SLOT. The martyr's exapostilarion (Spec. Mel. “Hearken, ye women ...”) is in `exapostilarion`. The forefeast exapostilarion occupies the 'Glory ..., Both now ...' slot and is stored in `matins_exapostilarion_theotokion` because that is the canonical Both-now carrier in this vocabulary — but it is NOT a theotokion in form: it is the forefeast exapostilarion ('Be renewed, O Adam ...'). Flagged, not renamed. BEATITUDES: 'On the Beatitudes, 8 Troparia: 4 from ODE III of the canon of the forefeast, and 4 from ODE VI of the canon of the holy martyr.' The PDF prints 7 unique texts (3 forefeast Ode III, 3 martyr Ode VI, plus the martyr's Ode VI Theotokion) with the FIRST marked (Twice), which fills the 8 slots. Encoded as 7 entries with the repeat carried as a `note` on the first, per the beatitudes convention. OCA CHECK — NO OVERRIDE APPLIED. The PDF's commemoration (Forefeast of the Nativity of the Theotokos + Martyr Sozon) is the same commemoration OCA lists as primary for 7 September, so oca_primary is true. No live OCA lookup was possible during this encoding pass (no network access), so no OCA text was compared hymn-by-hymn and no §2.1 step 5 override was applied; every text below is the St. Sergius thou/thy text, which governs per §11 #17 wherever the daily PDF prints a proper. PRINTED-SOURCE ANOMALIES, PRESERVED VERBATIM, NOT SILENTLY REPAIRED: (1) Sozon's ikos reads '... ever abideth with the faithful Wherefore, drawn by love ...' — the sentence break after 'faithful' is missing in the printed book. (2) The martyr's troparion reads 'In his sufferings, Thy martyr Sozon O Lord' with no comma after 'Sozon'. Both are defects in the printed source (§1.3 errata class), not extraction artifacts, and are left as printed. SCOPE: full canon odes (both canons), the two Sessional Hymns after the chantings of the Psalter, the Sessional Hymns after Ode III, and the Matins Aposticha (printed in Tone II, Spec. Mel. “O house of Ephratha ...”, with its own Tone II doxasticon) are out of scope at this rank per §6 and are deliberately not captured.",feast_e:`Ephesians 6:10-17 (§233)`,feast_g:`John 15:17-16:2 (§52)`,prokeimenon_tone:7,prokeimenon_text:`The righteous man shall be glad in the Lord, * and shall hope in Him.`,prokeimenon_stichos:`Hearken, O God, unto my prayer, when I make supplication unto Thee.`,alleluia_tone:4,alleluia_verse:`The righteous cried, and the Lord heard them, and He delivered them out of all their tribulations.`,alleluia_stichos:`Many are the tribulations of the righteous, and the Lord shall deliver them out of them all.`,communion_verse:`In everlasting remembrance shall the righteous be; he shall not be afraid of evil tidings.`,stichera_lord_i_call_count:6,stichera_lord_i_call:[{tone:1,spec_mel:`Joy of the ranks of heaven`,text:`Noetic beams of universal joy * have shone forth upon the world, * heralding Christ God, the Sun of righteousness unto all, * through thy nativity O most pure one. * For thou hast been revealed to be ** the mediatress of true gladness and grace.`},{tone:1,spec_mel:`Joy of the ranks of heaven`,text:`This, the glory of thy forefeast, O most pure one, * doth proclaim unto all the people * thy benefactions which transcend description; * for thou hast become for us the mediatress of gladness * and the cause of joy to come, ** O Ever-virgin Theotokos.`},{tone:1,spec_mel:`Joy of the ranks of heaven`,text:`The maiden who held God within her womb, * the pure Theotokos, * the glory of the prophets, the daughter of David * is born today of Joachim and the chaste Anna; * and the curse of Adam which lay upon us ** is annulled by her nativity.`},{tone:4,spec_mel:`Thou hast given a sign`,text:`When He Who of His own will * took our weakness upon Himself, * He strengthened thine infirmity with power, O blessed one, * for His sake thou didst run the race of martyrdom, rejoicing, * and with thy most beautiful feet * didst crush the impotent enemy. * Wherefore, Jesus, the Lover of mankind, * the Savior of our souls, ** hath woven wreaths of victory for thee.`},{tone:4,spec_mel:`Thou hast given a sign`,text:`When thou wast wounded, O glorious one, * thou didst wound thine enemies * and cast them down to the ground, * armed with the Cross * and manifestly arrayed in invincible armor, * made steadfast by the love * of Him Who hath translated thee to the noetic kingdoms * as an unvanquished martyr, ** and a heavenly man.`},{tone:4,spec_mel:`Thou hast given a sign`,text:`Manifestly emulating * the Savior and Lord * Who for thee underwent voluntary sufferings, * O spiritual athlete Sozon, * thou didst of thine own will give thyself over * to wounds and stripes and unbearable torments. * Wherefore, He hath honored thee with heavenly gifts * and showed thee forth as a well-spring of healings ** for those who love thee.`}],stichera_glory:{tone:4,text:`O Virgin most holy and pure, we, the multitude of angels in heaven and the race of mankind on earth, bless thy most honored nativity, in that thou didst become the Mother of Christ God, the Creator of all. Cease thou never to entreat Him, we pray, on behalf of us who, after God, have placed our trust in thee, O all-praised Theotokos who knewest not wedlock.`},stichera_aposticha:[{tone:8,spec_mel:`O most glorious wonder`,text:`O most glorious wonder! * The rod which is to bear the Flower * springeth forth today from the righteous Joachim and Anna, * from a barren mother * and a fruitless womb, O Mother of God * Who knewest not wedlock. * Wherefore, the council of the prophets * and the whole festive assembly of the patriarchs ** rejoice now in her nativity.`},{tone:8,spec_mel:`O most glorious wonder`,verse:`Hearken, O daughter, and see, * and incline thine ear.`,text:`Today David doth rejoice, * Jesse now danceth, * Levi is magnified, * and the righteous Joachim rejoiceth in spirit: * for the childlessness of Anna is loosed * by thy nativity, O most pure Mary, * divinely joyous one. * And all of us, the assembly of angels and men, ** call thy divine womb blessed.`},{tone:8,spec_mel:`O most glorious wonder`,verse:`The rich among the people * shall entreat thy countenance.`,text:`Rejoice, O assembly of men! Rejoice, O temple of the Lord! * Rejoice, holy mountain! Rejoice, divine table! * Rejoice, O lamp of the Light! * Rejoice, O honored glory of the Orthodox! * Rejoice, O Mary, Mother of Christ God! * Rejoice, O all-immaculate one! * Rejoice, offering of the Bread divine! * Rejoice, O tabernacle! Rejoice, O bush unburnt! ** Rejoice thou hope of all!`}],aposticha_glory:{tone:4,text:`Universal joy hath shone forth upon us from the righteous Joachim and Anna, O all-praised Virgin who, in thine exceeding purity, didst become the animate temple of God, and who alone art truly known to be the Theotokos. Through her prayers, O Christ God, grant peace unto the world and great mercy unto our souls.`},troparion:{tone:4,text:`In his sufferings, Thy martyr Sozon O Lord, * received an imperishable crown from Thee, our God; * for, possessed of Thy might, * he set at naught the tyrants and crushed the feeble audacity of the demons. ** By his supplications save Thou our souls.`},troparion_second:{tone:4,text:`Mary, the divine maiden, is born unto us today * from the root of Jesse and the loins of David; * and all things rejoice and are made new through joy. * Rejoice together, O heaven and earth! * Praise her, ye lands of the gentiles! * Joachim maketh glad, and Anna holdeth festival, crying aloud: ** A barren woman giveth birth to the Theotokos, the Nourisher of our Life!`},kontakion_ode3:{tone:2,spec_mel:`The steadfast`,text:`Assembling today, with a loud voice let us praise Sozon, * the true and divinely wise martyr, * the skilled spiritual athlete of piety, * the initiate of the mysteries of grace, * the most generous bestower of healings: * for he entreateth Christ God ** on behalf of us all.`},kontakion_ode6:{tone:3,spec_mel:`Today the Virgin`,text:`Today Mary, the Virgin and Theotokos, * the boundless chamber of the heavenly Bridegroom, is born! * From a barren woman, by the counsel of God, * the chariot of the Word is rightly adorned; ** for she was foretold as the divine portal and Mother of life.`},ikos_ode3:`Come ye, and with hymns let us all now earnestly praise Sozon offering worthy hymns unto the great spiritual athlete who hath shone forth in the honored and divine Church of Christ: for he truly laid low the savagery of the idolaters, and, bestowing salvation, ever abideth with the faithful Wherefore, drawn by love, O mortals, let us now call him blessed, honoring the precious and glorious memory of the spiritual athlete, and crying aloud: O all-blessed Sozon, entreat Christ God on behalf of us all!`,ikos:`The divine maiden Mary, whom once the prophets of God foresaw in the Spirit, hath been given as the fruit of a barren woman. Beholding her today leaping up in the womb of Anna, with the faithful Joachim we noetically make haste to the appointed feast, and, standing afar off, cry aloud, saying: The restoration of this world hath now sprouted forth from the womb of the barren woman: the divine portal and Mother of life!`,exapostilarion:`Armed with the weapon of thy precious Cross, O Word, Thy spiritual athlete soundly vanquished the hosts of the adversary and put the tyrants to shame; he suffered for Thee, O my Christ, King of all, and ever reigneth with Thee.`,matins_exapostilarion_theotokion:{text:`Be renewed, O Adam, and be thou magnified, O Eve! Ye prophets, join chorus with the apostles and the righteous! For the common joy of angels and mortals hath shone forth today from the righteous Joachim and Anna: Mary the Theotokos.`},beatitudes_source:`menaion`,beatitudes_count:8,beatitudes_troparia:[{text:`A fertile land is born from a barren land, bearing as fruit the Husbandman of all good things, the life-bearing Grain Who nourisheth all by His divine providence.`,source:`Ode III`,label:`Ode III, 1`,note:`(Twice) per PDF`},{text:`Today hath the rod of virginity sprouted forth, from whence our God, the Gardener, hath blossomed forth as a flower, cutting down the branches of wickedness by His extreme beauty.`,source:`Ode III`,label:`Ode III, 2`},{text:`Lo! the mountain unquarried, which arose from a barren rock, produceth the noetically quarried Stone, Who crusheth the delusion of all graven idols.`,source:`Ode III`,label:`Ode III, 3`},{text:`Desiring to be slain as an unblemished sacrifice, for the sake of God’s love, thou didst offer thyself up on His altar. Wherefore, we piously honor thee, O all-famed one.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI, 1`},{text:`Having sailed over the deep of deception without foundering, thou didst attain unto the calm haven of the kingdom on high, O blessed martyr, enjoying everlasting quietude.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI, 2`},{text:`The tormenter pitilessly raked thy sides with claws of iron; but thou didst gloriously commit thy spirit into the hands of God, O ever memorable and all-blessed one.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI, 3`},{text:`O wonder more recent than all wonders! The Virgin conceived in her womb, without knowing a man, Him Who sustaineth all things, yet did not confine Him.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI, Theotokion`}]},"09-08":[{saint:`The Nativity of Our Most Holy Sovereign Lady, the Theotokos and Ever-Virgin Mary`,oca_primary:!0,source_file:`09-08.pdf`,rank:`great_feast`,fekula_section:`2F`,has_great_doxology:!0,has_polyeleos:!0,has_litya:!0,has_paroemias:!0,magnificat_sung:!0,matins_format:`god_is_the_lord`,matins_gospel:`Luke 1:39-49, 56 (§4)`,aposticha_source:`menaion`,magnification:`We magnify thee, O most holy Virgin, we honor thy holy parents, and we glorify thy most glorious nativity.`,magnification_selected_psalm:`Remember, O Lord, David and all his meekness.`,note:"GREAT FEAST BY IDENTITY, not by rubric. encoding_rule_v2.md §1.1 step 1 makes the Great Feast test a closed enumerated list resolved by identity — 'Check identity, not the PDF rubric' — and 'her Nativity' is named on it. The waterfall stops at step 1; steps 2-6 were not run. ODS 3rd ed. vol. III carries no 09-08 entry, so §1.1 step 0 does not apply and there is no printed rank to weigh. NOTHING IN THE SOURCE CONTRADICTS THE CALL: the PDF prints AT LITTLE VESPERS (which would independently satisfy step 2's Vigil diagnostic), Polyeleos with Magnification (step 3), Litiya with blessing of the loaves, three Old Testament Lessons, and the Great Doxology (step 4). Every marker points the same direction. PAIRED WITH 09-14 — READ BOTH NOTES TOGETHER. The Exaltation of the Cross is also encoded rank: 'great_feast' even though ODS vol. III p.23 prints 'vigil-rank' for it, because vigil-rank is the FLOOR for the Twelve Great Feasts rather than a ceiling, and both feasts are already carried as rank: 'great' in FIXED_GREAT_FEASTS in hours-tool.jsx with the note 'One of the Twelve Great Feasts'. Ranking these two differently — great_feast here and vigil there — would have made the festal class depend on the accident of which dates vol. III happens to cover. OPEN RULE DEFECT, FLAGGED NOT FIXED: §1.1 step 1's enumerated list holds ELEVEN feasts (Master: Nativity, Theophany, Palm Sunday, Ascension, Pentecost, Transfiguration; Theotokos: her Nativity, Entrance, Meeting, Annunciation, Dormition). The Twelve Great Feasts include the Exaltation of the Cross, which the list omits. Nothing in the code keys on that list — the festal class is carried by FIXED_GREAT_FEASTS and the `season` layer, independent of any Menaion entry's rank — so this is a documentation gap rather than a live defect. Not silently corrected. LIC: 8 slots from 6 unique texts. Sergius the Patriarch supplies three (Tone VI, the third marked Twice); Stephen of Jerusalem three (Tone VI, the first marked Twice). Not a uniform multiple, so positional repeatIndex markers per §6b. All six are printed as unpointed prose — Tier 1 per §3.2, correct as printed, not a flattening. ZADOSTOINIK: the PDF prints 'Instead of It is truly meet..., we chant the Irmos of ODE IX of Canon I' with its own refrain, and adds 'This is done until the leave-taking of the feast.' That sentence is the source authority for the same zadostoinik on the 09-12 Leavetaking entry, which has no daily PDF of its own. SECOND COMMEMORATION: 09-08A.pdf prints a full polyeleos service for the Finding of the Kursk-Root Icon of the Sign, encoded as the second array element with oca_primary: false — it is a Russian/ROCOR commemoration absent from the OCA calendar for this date, per §1 OCA primacy. SCOPE: §6 restricts full canon odes, sessional hymns and Praises stichera to Great Feasts that the user requests BY DATE in that session. No such request was made, so the two canons (12 troparia), the three sessional sets and the four Praises stichera are present in the PDF and deliberately not captured.",feast_e:`Philippians 2:5-11 (§240)`,feast_g:`Luke 10:38-42; 11:27-28 (§54)`,prokeimenon_tone:3,prokeimenon_text:`The hymn of the Theotokos: My soul doth magnify the Lord, * and my spirit hath rejoiced in God my Savior.`,prokeimenon_stichos:`For He hath looked upon the lowliness of His handmaiden; for behold, from henceforth all generations shall call me blessed.`,alleluia_tone:8,alleluia_verse:`Hearken, O daughter, and see, and incline thine ear.`,alleluia_stichos:`The rich among the people shall entreat thy countenance.`,communion_verse:`I will take the cup of salvation, and I will call upon the name of the Lord.`,matins_prokeimenon:`I shall commemorate thy name * in every generation and generation. (Tone IV) Verse: Hearken, O daughter, and see, * and incline thine ear.`,paroemia_1:`Genesis — Jacob's ladder at Bethel, the gate of heaven (Genesis 28:10-17)`,paroemia_2:`Ezekiel — the shut eastern gate through which the Lord alone has entered (Ezekiel 43:27-44:4)`,paroemia_3:`Proverbs — Wisdom hath built a house for herself and set up seven pillars (Proverbs 9:1-11)`,stichera_lord_i_call_count:8,stichera_lord_i_call:[{tone:6,text:`Today God, Who resteth on noetic thrones, hath prepared for Himself a holy throne on earth. He Who hath established the heavens by His wisdom hath in His loving-kindness created an animate heaven. For the God of wonders, the Hope of the hopeless, hath caused His Mother to spring forth as a Life-bearing plant from a barren root. Glory be to Thee, O Lord!`},{tone:6,text:`This is the day of the Lord! Rejoice, O ye people! For lo! the bridal-chamber of the Light and the book of the Word of life hath issued forth from the womb, and the portal which faceth toward the east, having been born, awaiteth the entry of the great High Priest. She alone hath led the one Christ into the world, for the salvation of our souls.`},{tone:6,text:`Even though, by the will of God, famous barren women have produced offspring, yet in divine majesty hath Mary outshone all who have been born; for, having been most gloriously born of a barren mother, she supra-naturally gave birth in the flesh to the God of all, from a womb which knew not seed. She alone is the gate of the only-begotten Son of God; and He, passing through it, hath kept it closed, and, arranging all things wisely in accordance with His knowledge, hath wrought salvation for all mankind.`},{repeatIndex:2},{tone:6,text:`Today the gateway of the barren woman hath been opened, and the divine Virgin portal cometh forth! Today grace beginneth to bear fruit, revealing to the world the Mother of God, through whom those on earth are united to those in heaven, for the salvation of our souls.`},{repeatIndex:4},{tone:6,text:`Today is the pronouncement of universal joy! Today the winds have blown which herald salvation, and our nature is released from barrenness! For the barren woman is shown to be the mother of her who remaineth virgin even after giving birth to the Creator, from whom God taketh upon Himself that which is alien to Him by nature, and Christ, the Redeemer of our souls, the Lover of mankind, doth accomplish salvation for the lost by means of flesh.`},{tone:6,text:`Today barren Anna giveth birth to the divine maiden who was chosen beforehand out of all generations to be the dwelling-place of Christ our God, the King and Creator of all, in fulfillment of the divine dispensation. Thereby, O ye mortals, we have been fashioned anew and restored from corruption to endless life.`}],stichera_glory:{tone:6,text:`Today God, Who resteth on noetic thrones, hath prepared for Himself a holy throne on earth. He Who hath established the heavens by His wisdom hath in His loving-kindness created an animate heaven. For the God of wonders, the Hope of the hopeless, hath caused His Mother to spring forth as a Life-bearing plant from a barren root. Glory be to Thee, O Lord!`},litya_stichera:[{tone:1,text:`Today is the beginning of our salvation, O ye people! For, lo! the Virgin Mother, who was foretold from generations of old as the receptacle of God, cometh forth to be born from a barren woman. The flower of Jesse and the rod of his root have sprung forth. Let Adam our forefather rejoice, and let Eve revel in jubilation! For, behold! She who was fashioned from the rib of Adam manifestly blesseth her daughter and descendant, saying: “Deliverance hath been born within me, for which cause I have been freed of the bonds of Hades!” Let David rejoice, striking his harp, and let him bless God: for, lo! the Virgin issueth forth from the womb of the barren woman, unto the salvation of our souls!`},{tone:2,text:`Come, all ye who love virginity, ye zealots of purity! Come, and with love take up the praise of the Virgin: the well-spring of Life which floweth from the hardened stone, the bush which grew from the barren ground, unconsumed by the immaterial Fire, which purifieth and illumineth our souls.`},{tone:2,text:`What is this noise of revelers? Joachim and Anna keep festival mystically, saying: “Rejoice with us today, O Adam and Eve!” For by their transgression paradise was shut, but a right glorious fruit hath now been granted unto us: Mary, the divine maiden, the fruit which openeth wide its gates unto all.`},{tone:2,text:`The Queen of all who hath been foretold, the habitation of God, the divine dwelling place of the eternal Essence, hath come forth today from the barren womb of the glorious Anna, and by her, shameless Hades hath been trampled underfoot, and Eve, the mother of our race, hath been led into everlasting life. To her do we cry out, as is meet: Blessed art thou among women, and blessed is the fruit of thy womb!`}],litya_glory:{tone:8,text:`On the most excellent day of our feast let us strike the spiritual harp; for the Mother of Life is born today from the seed of David, dispelling darkness: the renewal of Adam, the restoration of Eve, the Well-spring of incorruption, our release from corruption. Because of her we have been deified and delivered from death. And we, the faithful, cry out to her with Gabriel: Rejoice, thou who art full of grace, the Lord is with thee, granting us great mercy for thy sake!`},litya_both_now:null,stichera_aposticha:[{tone:4,text:`The joy of the whole world hath shone forth upon us from the righteous Joachim and Anna: the most laudable Virgin who, because of her surpassing purity, hath become the animate temple of God and is known as the one true Theotokos. Through her prayers, O Christ God, send down peace upon the world and great mercy upon our souls.`},{tone:4,verse:`Hearken, O daughter, and see, * and incline thine ear.`,text:`In accordance with the angel’s prophecy, thou didst issue forth from the righteous Joachim and Anna, as a most pure fruit, heaven and the throne of God, a receptacle of purity, heralding forth joy to all the world, O Virgin, mediatress of our life, removal of the curse, bestowal of blessing. Wherefore, on the feast of thy nativity, O divinely called Virgin, ask peace for the world and great mercy for our souls.`},{tone:4,verse:`The rich among the people * shall entreat thy countenance.`,text:`Today let the barren and childless Anna clap her hands with splendor, let those on earth bear lamps, let kings leap for joy, let hierarchs be glad in blessing, and let all the world keep festival; for, behold, the Queen, the immaculate Bride of the Father, hath sprung forth from the root of Jesse. No longer will women bear children in grief, for Joy hath blossomed forth and Life shall live amongst mortals in the world. No longer will the offerings of Joachim be rejected, for the lamentation of Anna hath been changed to joy, and she saith: “Rejoice with me, all ye chosen Israel, for, lo! the Lord hath given me the animate palace of His divine glory, for our common gladness and joy, and the salvation of our souls!”`}],aposticha_glory:{tone:8,text:`Come, all ye faithful, let us make haste to the Virgin, for lo! she is born who was foreseen before the womb as the Mother of our God, the vessel of virginity, the rod of Aaron which sprang forth from the root of Jesse, the proclamation of the prophets, the offspring of the righteous Joachim and Anna! She is born, and with her the world hath been restored! She is born, and the Church adorneth herself in her majesty! She is the holy temple and receptacle of the Godhead, the vessel of virginity, the bridal chamber of the King, wherein was wrought the most glorious and perfect mystery of the ineffable union of the natures which have come together in Christ! And worshipping Him, we hymn the nativity of the all-immaculate Virgin.`},aposticha_both_now:null,troparion:{tone:4,text:`Thy nativity, O Virgin Theotokos, * hath proclaimed joy to all the world; * for from thee hath shone forth Christ our God, the Sun of righteousness, * Who, having annulled the curse, hath given His blessing, ** and having abolished death, hath granted us life everlasting.`},kontakion_ode6:{tone:4,text:`In thy holy nativity, O most pure one, * Joachim and Anna have been freed from the reproach of childlessness, * and Adam and Eve from mortal corruption. * And, delivered from the affliction of sin, * thy people celebrate it, crying out to thee: * A barren woman giveth birth to the Theotokos, * the nurturer of our Life!`},ikos:`The supplication of Joachim over his childlessness, together with the sighing of Anna over her barrenness, were right acceptable to God: they entered the ears of the Lord and brought forth life-bearing fruit for the world. For the one made supplication on the mountain, and the other bore her reproach in the garden; and with joy the barren woman giveth birth to the Theotokos, the nurturer of our Life.`,gospel_sticheron:{tone:6,text:`This is the day of the Lord! Rejoice, O ye people! For, lo! the bridal-chamber of the Light and the book of the Word of life hath issued forth from the womb, and the portal which faceth toward the east, having been born, awaiteth the entry of the great High Priest. She alone leadeth the one Christ into the world, for the salvation of our souls.`},exapostilarion:`Today the Theotokos doth issue forth like a flower from the barren Anna, instilling all the ends of the world with a most divine fragrance, filling all creation with joy. Hymning her, we praise her as is meet, as the one who is more exalted than all those earthborn.`,matins_exapostilarion_theotokion:{text:`Be thou renewed, O Adam! Be thou magnified, O Eve! Join ye chorus with the apostles and the righteous! For Mary the Theotokos, the common joy of the angels and mortals, hath shone forth today from the righteous Joachim and Anna.`},it_is_truly_meet_suppressed:!0,zadostoinik_refrain:`Magnify, O my soul, the most glorious nativity of the Mother of God!`,zadostoinik_irmos:`Thee do we magnify, O blessed and all-pure Theotokos, * who through thy virginal womb ineffably brought forth * God incarnate, * the Luminary Who shone forth before the sun * and hath come to us in the flesh.`,beatitudes_source:`menaion`,beatitudes_troparia:[{text:`Having lived blamelessly for God, ye gave birth unto the salvation of all, O divinely wise parents of her who hath given birth to our Creator and God.`,source:`Ode III`,label:`Canon I, Ode III, 1`,note:`(Twice) per PDF`},{text:`From a barren woman did the Lord, Who poureth forth life upon all, cause the Virgin to come forth, in whom He was pleased to make His abode, preserving her incorrupt even after giving birth.`,source:`Ode III`,label:`Canon I, Ode III, 2`},{text:`Let us hymn Mary today as the Theotokos, the fruit of Anna, the intercessor and helper of all, who gaveth birth to the life-bearing Cluster.`,source:`Ode III`,label:`Canon I, Ode III, 3`},{text:`We hymn thy holy nativity and honor thine immaculate conception of Christ, O divinely chosen Bride and Virgin. And with us the ranks of angels and the souls of the saints glorify thee.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Canon II, Ode VI, 1`},{text:`Thy chaste parents placed thee, who art holy among the saints, in the temple of the Lord, O pure one, to be raised with honor in preparation to become His Mother.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Canon II, Ode VI, 2`},{text:`In thee, O pure one, is the mystery of the Trinity hymned and glorified; for well pleased was the Father to have the Word make His abode within thee, and the divine Spirit to overshadow thee.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Canon II, Ode VI, Glory`},{text:`Thou wast a golden candlestick, O pure Birthgiver of God, for in thy womb the Fire made His abode: the Word from the Holy Spirit; and He became visible in thee in human form.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Canon II, Ode VI, Both now`}]},{saint:`Commemoration of the Finding of the Wonderworking Icon of the Most Holy Theotokos known as the Kursk-Root Icon of the Sign`,oca_primary:!1,source_file:`09-08A.pdf`,rank:`polyeleos`,fekula_section:`2E`,has_great_doxology:!1,has_polyeleos:!0,has_litya:!0,has_paroemias:!0,magnificat_sung:!0,matins_format:`god_is_the_lord`,matins_gospel:`Luke 1:39-49, 56`,aposticha_source:`menaion`,magnification:`Meet it is to magnify thee, O Theotokos, that art more honorable than the cherubim and beyond compare more glorious than the seraphim.`,magnification_selected_psalm:`O God, give Thy judgment to the king, and thy righteousness to the son of the king.`,matins_prokeimenon:`I shall commemorate thy name * in every generation and generation. (Tone IV) Verse: Hearken, O daughter, and see, and incline thine ear.`,note:"SECOND COMMEMORATION, NOT THE PRIMARY. 8 September is the Great Feast of the Nativity of the Theotokos; this entry is the Kursk-Root Icon service printed in 09-08A.pdf and is an array element behind that feast. `oca_primary` is false: the OCA calendar does not list the finding of the Kursk-Root Icon on 8 September at all — it is a Russian/ROCOR commemoration (the icon is the ROCOR Synod's own palladium), and per encoding_rule_v2.md §1 OCA primacy the OCA calendar governs which commemoration is primary, so this one is recorded as secondary. No OCA text exists for it, so no OCA override was available or applied and no OCA/St. Sergius divergence could be checked. RANK PER §1.1 WATERFALL, RUN IN FULL because ODS 3rd ed. vol. III does not cover this commemoration (there is no `ods3-0908A` entry among the 77), so §1.1 step 0 does not apply. Step 1 Great Feast — NO: the closed enumerated list names the Nativity of the Theotokos, which is the day's PRIMARY commemoration, not the finding of an icon; rank is a property of this commemoration's own service. Step 2 Vigil — NO: 09-08A.pdf prints no Small Vespers; the file opens directly at 'AT GREAT VESPERS'. Step 3 Polyeleos — YES: p.6 prints 'After the Polyeleos, this Magnification: Meet it is to magnify thee, O Theotokos...' followed by a Selected Psalm verse, and no Small Vespers precedes it. The waterfall stops here; steps 4-6 were not reached. VIGIL-LIKE FEATURES THAT DO NOT CHANGE THE RANK: the service prints 'Blessed is the man', eight stichera at Lord I Have Cried, three Lessons, and a full Litiya — all of which commonly accompany a vigil. §1.1 step 2's diagnostic is nevertheless the presence of a Small Vespers in the Menaion PDF, and there is none, so the service is polyeleos rank. Recorded rather than reconciled. ANOMALY — NO GREAT DOXOLOGY RUBRIC. The PDF appoints four Praises stichera with a Glory/Both now (p.13) and then breaks straight to 'AT LITURGY' on p.14: it prints no 'Great Doxology' rubric and no Matins dismissal. Praises stichera at a polyeleos service ordinarily lead into the Great Doxology (cf. 09-05, whose PDF prints the rubric outright), so its absence here is very likely an omission in the printed source rather than a rubrical direction. `has_great_doxology` is set FALSE because the rubric is not printed and inventing it is not permitted; the gap is flagged here rather than silently filled. For the same reason no Matins Aposticha is recorded — none is printed. KONTAKION ROUTING: the PDF prints exactly ONE kontakion, 'of the holy icon, in Tone VI' ('Come, ye faithful, let us radiantly celebrate...'), at the Ode VI position (p.10) and again under AT LITURGY (p.15) — the same text in both places, not two kontakia. Per §5.x a single kontakion is stored in `kontakion_ode6` and the assembler routes it to all four Hours; `kontakion_ode3` is therefore absent by rubric, not unencoded. The Ikos printed immediately after it ('Through thee, O Theotokos, the pre-eternal Word was made flesh...') is stored in `ikos`, which menaion-browser.jsx labels 'Ikos (after Ode VI)' — truthful here. LORD I HAVE CRIED: 8 slots from 8 UNIQUE texts — five in Tone VIII (Spec. Mel. 'O most glorious wonder', pointed with * and **) and three in Tone III (printed as unpointed prose in the source and kept that way). The array therefore mixes a pointed sub-group with a prose sub-group; that is what the source prints, and the validator's Check E groups by spec_mel, so the pointed spec-mel group and the label-less prose group are each internally consistent and the array passes — the same shape as 07-08 (Kazan Icon), which Check E's own comment cites as the legitimate case. No repeat markers are needed because nothing repeats. The Glory and the Both now are printed as ONE combined doxasticon ('Glory ..., Both now ..., in Tone I'), so it is stored once in `stichera_glory` and `lic_theotokion` is omitted rather than duplicated — the same treatment as 09-06's combined aposticha doxasticon. The Litiya and the Vespers Aposticha likewise print combined 'Glory ..., Both now ...' doxastica, stored once in `litya_glory` and `aposticha_glory` with the Both-now fields omitted for the same reason. PAROEMIA CITATIONS PARTLY ABSENT FROM THE PDF: the source prints chapter and verse only for the third lesson ('READING FROM THE PROVERBS (9:1-11)'). The first two headings read 'READING FROM THE BOOK OF GENESIS' and 'READING FROM THE BOOK OF THE PROPHET EZEKIEL' with no citation; the passages are identified here from their printed text (Jacob's ladder at Bethel; the closed east gate of the sanctuary) and the identification is labelled as such in each field rather than presented as printed. MAGNIFICAT — FLAGGED, NOT RESOLVED. `magnificat_sung` is true because the PDF prints no rubric suppressing the Magnificat at Ode IX and a polyeleos-rank service ordinarily sings it. In ACTUAL USE on 8 September the day is the Great Feast of the Nativity of the Theotokos, whose Ode IX carries the feast's megalynarion refrains and displaces the Magnificat; that is a property of the PRIMARY entry for the date, not of this service as printed. Recorded here so the divergence is visible rather than silently applied. AT LITURGY: the PDF prints no § numbers for either reading (Hebrews 9:1-7; Luke 10:38-42, 11:27-28) — the numbers are absent from the source, not omitted here. SCOPE: the full canon (Odes I-IX), the sessional hymns, the Psalm 50 sticheron and the four Praises stichera are printed in the PDF and are deliberately NOT captured, per §6 and the encoding scope for ranks below Great Feast.",feast_e:`Hebrews 9:1-7`,feast_g:`Luke 10:38-42; 11:27-28`,prokeimenon_tone:3,prokeimenon_text:`The hymn of the Theotokos: My soul doth magnify the Lord, * and my spirit hath rejoiced in God my Savior.`,prokeimenon_stichos:`For He hath looked upon the lowliness of His handmaiden; for behold, from henceforth all generations shall call me blessed.`,alleluia_tone:8,alleluia_verse:`Hearken, O daughter, and see, and incline thine ear.`,alleluia_stichos:`The rich among the people shall entreat thy countenance.`,communion_verse:`I shall receive the cup of salvation, and call upon the name of the Lord.`,paroemia_1:`Genesis — Jacob's ladder at Bethel, "this is the gate of heaven" (Genesis 28:10-17; identified from the printed text — the PDF heading reads only "READING FROM THE BOOK OF GENESIS", with no chapter and verse)`,paroemia_2:`Ezekiel — the closed east gate of the sanctuary by which the Lord God of Israel hath entered in (Ezekiel 43:27-44:4; identified from the printed text — the PDF heading reads only "READING FROM THE BOOK OF THE PROPHET EZEKIEL", with no chapter and verse)`,paroemia_3:`Proverbs — Wisdom hath builded her house, she hath hewn out her seven pillars (Proverbs 9:1-11, as printed)`,stichera_lord_i_call_count:8,stichera_lord_i_call:[{tone:8,spec_mel:`O most glorious wonder`,text:`O all-hymned Virgin, * immaculate Mother of God the Word, * the astonishment of angel, and help of Christians, * be thou unto us an unassailable wall * against the assaults of enemies, visible and invisible, * stretching forth unto thy Son thy hands which held God, * destroying by thine almighty supplication * the counsels of the impious, ** and revealing the sign of thy mercy unto us.`},{tone:8,spec_mel:`O most glorious wonder`,text:`O Lady Theotokos, our hope unashamed, * bowing the knee of our hearts at this time, * we cry to thee in compunction: * The Russian land, which of old was called thy home, * do thou snatch from the hands of the devil, * stretching forth unto thy Son thy hands which held God, * by thy supplication * consuming all the dominion of the enemy, ** and revealing the sign of thy mercy unto us.`},{tone:8,spec_mel:`O most glorious wonder`,text:`O thou joy of all joys! * Most sweet consolation of those who sorrow! * Surety of sinners! * Salvation of the faithful * Lead us to the heavenly Kingdom, * compensating for our weaknesses by thine intercession; * intercede for us before the dread judgment seat of Christ, * delivering us from everlasting torment, ** and revealing the sign of thy mercy unto us.`},{tone:8,spec_mel:`O most glorious wonder`,text:`O wondrous tidings! * O most glorious sight! * An icon of the Theotokos hid within a grove, * and pouring forth a spring of living water * ever flowing to life eternal! * Wherefore, having found it, let us form a chorus, * and, clapping our hands with gladness, let us cry: * Rejoice, O most blessed one, ** for thou hast come unto us who are paupers!`},{tone:8,spec_mel:`O most glorious wonder`,text:`Sanctified by the wood of the Cross, * the trees of the forest pay homage * to the immaculate Mother * of Him Who stretched forth His hands upon the Tree; * for they preserved * the image of thy most pure face, * that being wondrously discovered, * the Church might be robed in its comeliness, ** and the faithful may be partakers of divine grace.`},{tone:3,text:`O all ye generations of the earth, bless Mary the Theotokos: Ye righteous and sinners, form one choir for her; ye virgins, hymn the ever-Virgin, the boast of virginity; ye mothers, praise the Mother of our Creator and God; ye elders, bow down before her who gave flesh to the Ancient of Days; ye babes, glorify her who hath held the pre-eternal Infant in her arms; ye poor, magnify her who doth possess a wealth of mercy and grace; ye kings, exalt the Queen of heaven and earth; and kissing her precious image, let us cry aloud: Rejoice, O inexhaustible joy and salvation of our souls!`},{tone:3,text:`O Lady, thy most precious icon hath appeared as a sign of thy good pleasure unto us, whereat, bending down as to a life-bearing spring, we draw forth the water of immortality, and freely receive divers healings; and worshiping thee, the prototype, we cry in faith: O most holy Theotokos, save us!`},{tone:3,text:`O most pure Virgin: the light, joy and protection of our souls, thou art the estrangement from sorrows and remission of sins, the fulfillment of humble requests and ever-flowing fount of grace, O immaculate one. Wherefore, falling down before thee, we pray: Cease not to cry to thy Son and God for us, that being delivered by thee from misfortunes, we may obtain great mercy from Christ the Lord.`}],stichera_glory:{tone:1,text:`Forming a most festive choir today, come ye, let us hymn the Virgin Theotokos; and, kissing her most pure image, let us cry: O most holy Lady, deliver thine inheritance from cruel enemies, and strengthen the faithful in the doing of good, entreating thy Son and God for all.`},litya_stichera:[{tone:1,text:`Come, all ye peoples of our native land, let us hasten to the temple of the Lord; let us praise the all-hymned Mother of our God, and bow down before her most pure image in faith, for it hath now been given to the Russian land as a pledge of salvation. Wherefore, radiantly keeping the feast thereof, let us unceasingly cry out to the Theotokos: Rejoice, ready help of the world, thou joy and salvation of our souls!`},{tone:1,text:`Laying aside all earthly care, and forsaking the vanity of the world, let us lift up our eyes unto the holy hill, from whence cometh our help; for lo! the Queen of heaven, standing at the right hand of her Son and God, doth pray without ceasing. Wherefore, let us, who have been delivered from misfortune by her intercessions, be glad, and say: Rejoice, our fervent helper, thou salvation of our souls!`},{tone:2,text:`Let us be filled today with great joy, and giving thanks with compunction let us cry out in fear and trembling: Why is this granted to us, that the Mother of our God hath come to us? For lo! her healing icon abideth in our midst, and we dare to touch it, to bow down before it, to kiss it, and to offer entreaty to its prototype, as to one that is truly with us. Wherefore, marveling at her condescension, we cry: Rejoice, O Virgin Theotokos, full of grace!`},{tone:7,text:`Come, all ye that labor and are heavy laden; come, ye who weep and mourn; come, ye who have lacked patience; come, ye poor and afflicted! Fall down before the icon of the most pure Virgin, and receive ye, each according to his need; for lo! the all-hymned Theotokos giveth all to each abundantly. She is the healing of the infirm, the most sweet consolation of those who grieve, the forgiveness of sinners and the salvation of all who have recourse to her in faith.`}],litya_glory:{tone:6,text:`When the fullness of time shall come; when the trumpet of the archangel shall sound; then the thrones of judgment shall be set, and the rivers of fire shall make a fearsome noise; the dead will arise and the living shall be caught up, and the sign of the Son of man shall appear in heaven, all the inhabitants of the earth shall weep, and all the powers of heaven shall be shaken. Then shall the Virgin Theotokos, the salvation of sinners and hope of the hopeless, appear, standing at the right hand of the Judge, stretching forth her most pure hands to her Son, mercifully bedewing her cheeks with tears, and delivering from everlasting torment those who call upon her all-hymned name. Therefore, brethren, let us now make haste, and delay not in approaching the Theotokos, that she, upon whom we place all our hope, may help us at that dreadful hour.`},stichera_aposticha:[{tone:1,text:`Truly thou pourest forth rivers of grace from thine icon, O Theotokos! For lo! even at the site of its discovery, a spring of healing water hath appeared. Thou dost quench the spiritual thirst of all who have recourse to thee in love, washing away sins, and granting us to draw forth joy and gladness.`},{tone:1,verse:`Hearken, O daughter, and see, * and incline thine ear.`,text:`At the root of a tree the image of her that budded forth the Tree of life was found. Wherefore, on the site of its appearance, a monastery was planted, like a spiritual orchard, wherein the all-hymned name of its prototype was glorified, supplications for the whole world were ceaselessly offered up, and men worked out their salvation with fear.`},{tone:1,verse:`The Lord hath sworn in truth unto David, * and He will not annul it.`,text:`Having the warriors of heaven as aides, and the Virgin Theotokos as an invincible commander, let us array ourselves against the enemies of our salvation, armed with the precious Cross, crying out to the most blessed one: Deprive us not of thy mighty assistance, for we have placed our hope on thee, that we be not put to shame forever.`}],aposticha_glory:{tone:5,text:`O Virgin Theotokos, how much more powerful than the hands of Moses are thy most pure hands, ever raised to God on our behalf? Wherefore, thou dost vanquish an enemy more powerful than Amalek, routing the very prince of darkness and his minions, granting victory to those who piously war against the passions, and showing them forth as heirs of the new promised land, wherein righteousness doth dwell.`},troparion:{tone:4,text:`Having acquired thee as an unassailable rampart * and a fount of miracles, * we, thy servants, set at naught armies of adversaries, * O most pure Theotokos. * Wherefore, do we entreat thee: * Grant peace to our native land ** and great mercy to our souls!`},kontakion_ode6:{tone:6,text:`Come, ye faithful, let us radiantly celebrate * the wondrous appearance of the most precious image of the Mother of God, * and drawing grace therefrom, * let us cry out with compunction: * Rejoice, O Theotokos, ** blessed Mary, Mother of God!`},ikos:`Through thee, O Theotokos, the pre-eternal Word was made flesh; through thee men have beheld the Sun of righteousness, upon Whom the angels dare not gaze: for in thine arms was borne Him Whom the cherubim bear aloft, and by thy most pure hands was held the Almighty Creator of all. O daughter of mortal Adam, thou wast called the one who gaveth birth to God. Wherefore, as one who alone hath maternal boldness before Him, pause not in thy prayers for the whole world, lifting up thy God-pleasing hands to thy Son, and inclining His love for mankind towards thy servants who cry out to thee with thankful mouths: Rejoice, O Theotokos, blessed Mary, Mother of God!`,exapostilarion:`O Virgin Theotokos, thou most radiant sun, kindle the inextinguishable beacon of the faith of Christ in our land by thy grace, that thereby light may be cast upon darkness and the hearts of the faithful may rejoice therein, giving thanks to Christ, the Lover of mankind, and granting to all great mercy.`,matins_exapostilarion_theotokion:{tone:3,text:`By my sins have I put aside the robe of baptism. How can I enter in unto the banquet of the Lord, having no wedding garment, if thou, O Theotokos, dost not cover me with thy mantle and utter a word to the King of glory on my behalf, wretch that I am? Wherefore, placing my hope on thee alone, I stretch forth my hands to thee: Do thou hearken, and protect and help me!`},beatitudes_source:`menaion`,beatitudes_troparia:[{text:`The eyes of our mind rushing headlong into the abyss of the ages, we see there thine icon, O most pure one, shining more brightly than the sun with rays of mercy, illumining thy home, the Russian land, O Theotokos. Wherefore, we cry unto thee: Rejoice, O our protection and defense!`,source:`Ode III`,label:`Ode III, 1`},{text:`In its bosom the dark oak of Kursk kept the radiant wonder, the most pure icon of the Mother of God, which had been cut in twain by the impious and grew back together through the power of God. Wherefore, recovering it like a great treasure, the Russian people cried out: Rejoice, O our protection and defense!`,source:`Ode III`,label:`Ode III, 2`},{text:`Whence is this, that the most blessed Mother of God cometh unto us? A spring of living water sprang forth at the place where the image of the Theotokos abode, refreshing the faithful with streams of healing and curing every wound and infirmity. Wherefore, we cry aloud to her who is full of grace: Rejoice, thou true life-bearing fount!`,source:`Ode III`,label:`Ode III, 3`},{text:`At the root of a tree was discovered the icon of her who ineffably gave rise to the Tree of life and budded forth for the world the blessed Fruit. Wherefore, now celebrating the feast of its appearance, we cry out to the most pure one: O most blessed Lady, sever our evil passions at the root, implanting in us good habits, setting out a garden of virtues in us, granting us to be partakers of the life of paradise!`,source:`Ode III`,label:`Ode III, 4`},{text:`Let despondency depart from us, O brethren, and let the showers of adverse thoughts be dispelled by the Spirit of God; for lo! the Mother of God herself doth openly dwell amongst us, worshipped in her wonderworking icon, and shining brighter than the sun in the miracles that proceed therefrom. Wherefore, we cry out from the depths of our hearts: O most holy Lady, ever abide with us in thy grace!`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI, 1`},{text:`When temptation doth assail us, when we fall into sorrow and pain, when patience doth fail within us and our spirit is troubled, let us make haste to the image of the most holy Virgin and, pouring forth tears, cry out to her from our hearts: Stretch forth thy hands which held God unto us, and lead us up from the abyss of evil!`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI, 2`},{text:`O thou who didst bear the everlasting Joy of the world, thou art the joy of joys who hast driven away the grief of sin and dost grant our troubled souls divine relief. Cease not thy supplications for the Christian world, for, after God, it is through thee that each is saved and led forth from the depths of offenses.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI, 3`},{text:`Lo! the hordes of evil demons that besiege Christians stand far off, watching, not daring to approach, fearing the glory of the icon of the Theotokos, which burneth the vile demons, but doth mightily aid the faithful, protecting them from all enemies.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI, 4`}]}],"09-12":{saint:`Leavetaking of the Nativity of the Most Holy Theotokos`,oca_primary:!0,source_file:`NOT IN PDF — no 09-12.pdf exists in the St. Sergius set; texts drawn from 09-08.pdf and the OCA calendar`,rank:`six_stichera`,fekula_section:`2G3`,fekula_section_override:`2G3`,has_great_doxology:!1,has_polyeleos:!1,has_litya:!1,has_paroemias:!1,magnificat_sung:!0,matins_format:`god_is_the_lord`,aposticha_source:`menaion`,note:`SYNTHESIZED ENTRY — NO SOURCE PDF. There is no 09-12.pdf in the St. Sergius set. This was verified directly against the mounted folder, not assumed: September is otherwise complete (38 files, 09-01 through 09-30 with A variants) and 12 is the only missing day. It is the second confirmed gap of its kind after 08-23.pdf (Apodosis of the Dormition). BOTH MISSING FILES ARE APODOSES, which is suggestive — but 09-21.pdf (Apodosis of the Exaltation) IS present, so this is an observation about two files, not a rule about apodoses. Recorded as observed, not generalized. PROVENANCE OF EVERY FIELD BELOW. The hymnography is the feast's own, taken verbatim from 09-08.pdf, because an Apodosis repeats the propers of the feast it closes (Fekula §2G3). The commemoration, the Saturday-before-Elevation identification and the Liturgy readings come from the OCA calendar for 12 September 2026. NOTHING in this entry is attributed to a 09-12.pdf, and source_file says so explicitly rather than naming a file that does not exist — per §12's provenance check and §11 #12. THE ZADOSTOINIK HAS DIRECT SOURCE AUTHORITY. 09-08.pdf, after the Ode IX irmos of Canon I, prints: 'This is done until the leave-taking of the feast.' That sentence is an instruction from the feast's own service TO this date, so the zadostoinik and its refrain are not inferred here — they are appointed. OCA GIVES THIS DATE THREE READING PAIRS, not one, because two special observances coincide with the ordinary Saturday: (1) the Leavetaking itself, which repeats the feast's Liturgy readings (Philippians 2:5-11, Luke 10:38-42; 11:27-28); (2) SATURDAY BEFORE ELEVATION, a lectionary day in its own right (First Corinthians 2:6-9, Matthew 10:37-11:1); and (3) the ordinary Saturday reading (First Corinthians 4:17-5:5, Matthew 24:1-13). feast_e/feast_g carry the feast's pair; the other two are recorded here because V1 has no field for a Saturday-before-Elevation lectionary overlay. That overlay is a real gap the assembler cannot currently express. ALSO COMMEMORATED per OCA: Hieromartyr Autonomus, Bishop in Italy (313); Ven. Vassian of Tiksnensk; Translation of the Relics of Righteous Simeon of Verkhoturye; Martyr Julian of Galatia and forty Martyrs with him; Hieromartyr Theodore of Alexandria; St. Coronatus of Nicomedia; Ven. Afanasy of Vysotsk. None has proper hymnography here — the day is governed by the Leavetaking — and no daily PDF exists from which to draw any. Not encoded rather than invented. RANK: six_stichera / §2G3 (Apodosis). The Apodosis keeps the feast's hymnography but drops the Vigil furniture — no Polyeleos, no Litiya, no Lessons, no Great Doxology — which is what distinguishes it from the feast day itself at 09-08. Because there is no PDF, this rank is derived from the Fekula §2G3 pattern rather than read off a printed rubric, and that distinction is stated here rather than hidden. IF A 09-12.pdf IS EVER DELIVERED, this entry should be re-encoded from it and this note replaced. It is a serviceable reconstruction, not a transcription.`,feast_e:`Philippians 2:5-11 (§240) — of the feast, repeated at its Leavetaking`,feast_g:`Luke 10:38-42; 11:27-28 (§54) — of the feast, repeated at its Leavetaking`,prokeimenon_tone:3,prokeimenon_text:`The hymn of the Theotokos: My soul doth magnify the Lord, * and my spirit hath rejoiced in God my Savior.`,prokeimenon_stichos:`For He hath looked upon the lowliness of His handmaiden; for behold, from henceforth all generations shall call me blessed.`,alleluia_tone:8,alleluia_verse:`Hearken, O daughter, and see, and incline thine ear.`,alleluia_stichos:`The rich among the people shall entreat thy countenance.`,communion_verse:`I will take the cup of salvation, and I will call upon the name of the Lord.`,stichera_lord_i_call_count:6,stichera_lord_i_call:[{tone:6,text:`Today God, Who resteth on noetic thrones, hath prepared for Himself a holy throne on earth. He Who hath established the heavens by His wisdom hath in His loving-kindness created an animate heaven. For the God of wonders, the Hope of the hopeless, hath caused His Mother to spring forth as a Life-bearing plant from a barren root. Glory be to Thee, O Lord!`},{tone:6,text:`This is the day of the Lord! Rejoice, O ye people! For lo! the bridal-chamber of the Light and the book of the Word of life hath issued forth from the womb, and the portal which faceth toward the east, having been born, awaiteth the entry of the great High Priest. She alone hath led the one Christ into the world, for the salvation of our souls.`},{tone:6,text:`Even though, by the will of God, famous barren women have produced offspring, yet in divine majesty hath Mary outshone all who have been born; for, having been most gloriously born of a barren mother, she supra-naturally gave birth in the flesh to the God of all, from a womb which knew not seed. She alone is the gate of the only-begotten Son of God; and He, passing through it, hath kept it closed, and, arranging all things wisely in accordance with His knowledge, hath wrought salvation for all mankind.`},{tone:6,text:`Today the gateway of the barren woman hath been opened, and the divine Virgin portal cometh forth! Today grace beginneth to bear fruit, revealing to the world the Mother of God, through whom those on earth are united to those in heaven, for the salvation of our souls.`},{tone:6,text:`Today is the pronouncement of universal joy! Today the winds have blown which herald salvation, and our nature is released from barrenness! For the barren woman is shown to be the mother of her who remaineth virgin even after giving birth to the Creator, from whom God taketh upon Himself that which is alien to Him by nature, and Christ, the Redeemer of our souls, the Lover of mankind, doth accomplish salvation for the lost by means of flesh.`},{tone:6,text:`Today barren Anna giveth birth to the divine maiden who was chosen beforehand out of all generations to be the dwelling-place of Christ our God, the King and Creator of all, in fulfillment of the divine dispensation. Thereby, O ye mortals, we have been fashioned anew and restored from corruption to endless life.`}],stichera_glory:{tone:6,text:`Today God, Who resteth on noetic thrones, hath prepared for Himself a holy throne on earth. He Who hath established the heavens by His wisdom hath in His loving-kindness created an animate heaven. For the God of wonders, the Hope of the hopeless, hath caused His Mother to spring forth as a Life-bearing plant from a barren root. Glory be to Thee, O Lord!`},stichera_aposticha:[{tone:4,text:`The joy of the whole world hath shone forth upon us from the righteous Joachim and Anna: the most laudable Virgin who, because of her surpassing purity, hath become the animate temple of God and is known as the one true Theotokos. Through her prayers, O Christ God, send down peace upon the world and great mercy upon our souls.`},{tone:4,verse:`Hearken, O daughter, and see, * and incline thine ear.`,text:`In accordance with the angel’s prophecy, thou didst issue forth from the righteous Joachim and Anna, as a most pure fruit, heaven and the throne of God, a receptacle of purity, heralding forth joy to all the world, O Virgin, mediatress of our life, removal of the curse, bestowal of blessing. Wherefore, on the feast of thy nativity, O divinely called Virgin, ask peace for the world and great mercy for our souls.`},{tone:4,verse:`The rich among the people * shall entreat thy countenance.`,text:`Today let the barren and childless Anna clap her hands with splendor, let those on earth bear lamps, let kings leap for joy, let hierarchs be glad in blessing, and let all the world keep festival; for, behold, the Queen, the immaculate Bride of the Father, hath sprung forth from the root of Jesse. No longer will women bear children in grief, for Joy hath blossomed forth and Life shall live amongst mortals in the world. No longer will the offerings of Joachim be rejected, for the lamentation of Anna hath been changed to joy, and she saith: “Rejoice with me, all ye chosen Israel, for, lo! the Lord hath given me the animate palace of His divine glory, for our common gladness and joy, and the salvation of our souls!”`}],aposticha_glory:{tone:8,text:`Come, all ye faithful, let us make haste to the Virgin, for lo! she is born who was foreseen before the womb as the Mother of our God, the vessel of virginity, the rod of Aaron which sprang forth from the root of Jesse, the proclamation of the prophets, the offspring of the righteous Joachim and Anna! She is born, and with her the world hath been restored! She is born, and the Church adorneth herself in her majesty! She is the holy temple and receptacle of the Godhead, the vessel of virginity, the bridal chamber of the King, wherein was wrought the most glorious and perfect mystery of the ineffable union of the natures which have come together in Christ! And worshipping Him, we hymn the nativity of the all-immaculate Virgin.`},troparion:{tone:4,text:`Thy nativity, O Virgin Theotokos, * hath proclaimed joy to all the world; * for from thee hath shone forth Christ our God, the Sun of righteousness, * Who, having annulled the curse, hath given His blessing, ** and having abolished death, hath granted us life everlasting.`},kontakion_ode6:{tone:4,text:`In thy holy nativity, O most pure one, * Joachim and Anna have been freed from the reproach of childlessness, * and Adam and Eve from mortal corruption. * And, delivered from the affliction of sin, * thy people celebrate it, crying out to thee: * A barren woman giveth birth to the Theotokos, * the nurturer of our Life!`},ikos:`The supplication of Joachim over his childlessness, together with the sighing of Anna over her barrenness, were right acceptable to God: they entered the ears of the Lord and brought forth life-bearing fruit for the world. For the one made supplication on the mountain, and the other bore her reproach in the garden; and with joy the barren woman giveth birth to the Theotokos, the nurturer of our Life.`,exapostilarion:`Today the Theotokos doth issue forth like a flower from the barren Anna, instilling all the ends of the world with a most divine fragrance, filling all creation with joy. Hymning her, we praise her as is meet, as the one who is more exalted than all those earthborn.`,matins_exapostilarion_theotokion:{text:`Be thou renewed, O Adam! Be thou magnified, O Eve! Join ye chorus with the apostles and the righteous! For Mary the Theotokos, the common joy of the angels and mortals, hath shone forth today from the righteous Joachim and Anna.`},it_is_truly_meet_suppressed:!0,zadostoinik_refrain:`Magnify, O my soul, the most glorious nativity of the Mother of God!`,zadostoinik_irmos:`Thee do we magnify, O blessed and all-pure Theotokos, * who through thy virginal womb ineffably brought forth * God incarnate, * the Luminary Who shone forth before the sun * and hath come to us in the flesh.`},"09-13":{saint:`Commemoration of the Dedication of the Church of the Holy Resurrection of Christ our God; Forefeast of the Elevation of the Precious & Life-creating Cross; Holy Hieromartyr Cornelius the Centurion`,oca_primary:!0,source_file:`09-13.pdf`,rank:`doxology`,fekula_section:`2D`,has_great_doxology:!0,has_polyeleos:!1,has_litya:!1,has_paroemias:!0,magnificat_sung:!0,matins_format:`god_is_the_lord`,aposticha_source:`menaion`,note:"RANK IS PRINTED, NOT INFERRED. ODS 3rd ed. vol. III (Services of the Menaion), pp. 17-19, entry `ods3-0913-sun`, states 'A doxology-rank service on Sunday'. Per encoding_rule_v2.md §1.1 STEP 0 a printed rank is decisive and short-circuits the waterfall; steps 1-6 were NOT run and must not be run for this date. 13 September 2026 is a Sunday, so the `-sun` entry governs; the parallel `ods3-0913-wkd` entry (pp. 20-22) prints 'A doxology-rank service on Monday through Saturday' — the same rank, so the Sunday/weekday choice does not affect what is recorded here. KONTAKION ROUTING BY PRINTED ODE POSITION (§5). The PDF prints the forefeast kontakion (Tone IV, spec. mel. 'Thou hast appeared') after Ode III → kontakion_ode3 → 1st & 6th Hours; and the Dedication kontakion (Tone II, spec. mel. 'The steadfast') with its Ikos after Ode VI → kontakion_ode6 + ikos → 3rd & 9th Hours. Both are reprinted AT LITURGY in that same order (forefeast first, Dedication second), which corroborates the positions independently of the Matins layout. All three §5.x positions (AT LITURGY, Ode VI, Ode III) were checked. SIX DIVERGENCES FROM ODS vol. III, all flagged rather than reconciled. Vol. III describes the SUNDAY-LAYERED service; the St. Sergius daily PDF prints the saint's own service. Per §1.1's governing principle rank is a property of the commemoration, and Sunday layering is the assembler's job, not this entry's. (1) MATINS KONTAKION SLOTS: vol. III p.18 puts the kontakion and ikos OF THE DEDICATION after Ode III and the kontakion and ikos OF THE RESURRECTION from the Octoechos after Ode VI, and its Hours rubric (p.19) accordingly gives the Dedication kontakion at the 1st & 6th Hours and the resurrection kontakion at the 3rd & 9th. This entry stores the PDF's own positions. (2) LITTLE VESPERS AND LITIYA: vol. III prints both for the Sunday; the daily PDF prints neither, and vol. III's own rubric adds 'If a vigil is not served, the Litia is generally omitted'. has_litya is false on the PDF's authority. Little Vespers is out of V1 scope regardless (no field vocabulary). (3) Vol. III's Litia doxasticon (Tone V, 'O Word, who restest in the bosom of the Father...') does not appear in the PDF at all. (4) LIC COUNT: vol. III appoints 10 stichera on the Sunday (4 of the resurrection + 3 of the Dedication + 3 of the forefeast drawn from the Vespers Aposticha); the PDF prints 6 (3 of the dedication + 3 of Cornelius). Encoded as the PDF prints. Note that vol. III's three forefeast LIC stichera ARE the three Aposticha stichera captured here. (5) CORNELIUS: vol. III transfers his canon and stichera to Little Compline on the Sunday; the PDF prints his 3 stichera in the main Vespers, encoded as printed. (6) BEATITUDES: vol. III appoints 10 troparia (6 of the resurrection + 4 from Ode III of the Dedication canon); the PDF appoints 8 (4 from Ode III and 4 from Ode VI of the Dedication canon), encoded as printed. OCA CHECK — TWO DIVERGENCES, no override applied. OCA's 13 September primary commemoration is 'Commemoration of the Founding of the Church of the Resurrection (Holy Sepulchre) at Jerusalem', listed with the Forefeast of the Elevation and Hieromartyr Cornelius — the same three commemorations the PDF heads. (a) KONTAKION ATTRIBUTION: OCA assigns the hymn 'The Church has been revealed to be a heaven filled with light ... Establish this house, O Lord' (Tone 4) to the DEDICATION, and gives the Forefeast a different kontakion ('Your precious Cross rises like the sun ...') which the PDF does not print at all. The St. Sergius PDF labels that same hymn 'Kontakion of the Forefeast, in Tone IV'. Its content ('Establish this house') reads as a Dedication text, so the PDF's label appears to be a labeling defect in the printed source. Recorded as printed with the divergence flagged: §5 routes by printed ODE POSITION, not by label, so the Hours routing is unaffected either way. (b) CORNELIUS TROPARION: OCA's is 'O Blessed Cornelius, you were distinguished in works of righteousness ...'; the PDF's is the common hieromartyr text 'As thou didst share in the ways of the apostles ...', which OCA assigns on the same date to Hieromartyr Julian of Galatia. The PDF's proper stands per §2.1 (a proper exists in the daily PDF); the OCA text is recorded here only, not encoded. (c) OCA's Dedication troparion is the same hymn as the PDF's in contemporary register; per §11 #17 the thou/thy PDF text governs. FIELD-NAME MISMATCHES, FLAGGED NOT INVENTED AROUND (§12 rule). Three Both-now slots on this date hold FOREFEAST OF THE CROSS texts, not theotokia: the Vespers 'Both now' (stored in `lic_theotokion`, the canonical Both-now slot field), the Vespers Aposticha 'Both now' (`aposticha_both_now`, a neutral name), and the 'Both now' troparion (`troparion_bothnow`). `lic_stavrotheotokion` was considered and rejected — these are Cross stichera of the forefeast, not Stavrotheotokia. No field names were invented. POINTING: the three Dedication LIC stichera, both Vespers doxastica, all three Aposticha stichera and the Ikos are printed WITHOUT St. Sergius pointing (plain prose); the three Cornelius stichera, all four troparia, both kontakia and the exapostilaria ARE pointed with * and **. Stored exactly as printed — the absence of asterisks is the source's own layout, not a flattening. RUBRICAL CONDITIONAL recorded, not encoded: the PDF prints 'But if it be Friday evening: Both now ..., the Dogmaticon of the current tone.' 13 September 2026 is a Sunday, so this does not fire; the forefeast Both now stands. MATINS 'God is the Lord' prints the Dedication troparion marked (Twice), then Glory (Cornelius), Both now (forefeast) — the same three troparia stored below. ABSENT BY RANK, NOT BY OMISSION: no Polyeleos, no Magnification, no Matins Gospel and no Matins prokeimenon are appointed at doxology rank, so those fields are omitted rather than blanked. There is likewise no gospel_sticheron (no Matins Gospel to follow) and no Matins Aposticha — the Praises run directly into the Great Doxology (PDF p.19), which displaces it. PAROEMIAS: the PDF prints all three lessons under BARE headings with no chapter or verse citation ('A READING FROM THE THIRD BOOK OF KINGS', 'A READING FROM PROVERBS' ×2). The citations recorded below come from ODS vol. III p.17, which prints them explicitly, and match the texts as printed. SCOPE: the three full canons (Dedication, forefeast, Cornelius), the katavasiae, the sessional hymns and the six Praises stichera with their doxastica are all present in the PDF and are deliberately NOT captured, per §6, which puts them out of scope at every rank below Great Feast.",feast_e:`Hebrews 3:1-4 (§307)`,feast_g:`Matthew 16:13-18 (§67)`,prokeimenon_tone:4,prokeimenon_text:`Holiness becometh Thy house, O Lord, * unto length of days.`,prokeimenon_stichos:`The Lord is King, He is clothed with majesty.`,alleluia_tone:2,alleluia_verse:`His foundations are in the holy mountains.`,alleluia_stichos:`Glorious things are spoken of thee, O city of God.`,communion_verse:`O Lord, I have loved the beauty of Thy house, and the place where Thy glory dwelleth.`,paroemia_1:`Third Book of Kings (= First Kings in the Hebrew numbering) — Solomon's prayer at the dedication of the temple (Third Kings 8:22-23, 27-30). The PDF prints only the bare heading 'A READING FROM THE THIRD BOOK OF KINGS'; the citation is from ODS vol. III p.17, not from the PDF.`,paroemia_2:`Proverbs — “God by wisdom founded the earth” (Proverbs 3:19-34). The PDF prints only the bare heading 'A READING FROM PROVERBS'; the citation is from ODS vol. III p.17, not from the PDF.`,paroemia_3:`Proverbs — “Wisdom hath built a house for herself, and set up seven pillars” (Proverbs 9:1-11). The PDF prints only the bare heading 'A READING FROM PROVERBS'; the citation is from ODS vol. III p.17, not from the PDF.`,stichera_lord_i_call_count:6,stichera_lord_i_call:[{tone:6,text:`In goodly fashion doth the old law mandate that dedications be honored, and all the more should the new law also be honored with dedications; for the islands are made new for God, as saith Isaiah, by which we should understand the Churches now built by the nations, which receive a firm foundation from God. Wherefore, let us spiritually celebrate these present festivities.`},{tone:6,text:`Be ye renewed, O brethren, and, having put away the old man, live in newness of life, bridling all things that lead to death; let us therefore chastise all our members, hating every fruit of the tree of evil, and taking care to keep this in mind, let us avoid the ancient error, that mankind may be restored. Therefore, brethren, let us honor the day of dedication.`},{tone:6,text:`The composition of Anatolius: Thou didst set up Thy Church as a tower of strength, O Christ, Thou pre-eternal Word, for Thou didst found it upon the rock of the Faith. Wherefore, it abideth unshakable forever, having Thee Who, for its sake, immutably became man in latter times. Wherefore, giving thanks, we hymn Thee, saying: Thou art our King from before time began, throughout ages past, and still those to come: Glory be to Thee!`},{tone:4,spec_mel:`Thou hast given a sign`,text:`Beholding thy good works * and thy divine supplications, O Cornelius, * Christ sent a holy angel unto thee * to wholly enlighten thee, * and the foremost of the sacred apostles, * who renewed thee with water and the Spirit * with all thy household, O right glorious one, * teaching thee sublime things ** through the grace of the Spirit.`},{tone:4,spec_mel:`Thou hast given a sign`,text:`Anointed with the oil of the priesthood, * thou didst hasten to announce to all lands * the preaching of salvation, * uprooting the thorns of falsehood, O divinely wise one, * and planting the true doctrine in men’s souls through the Spirit. * Wherefore, in gladness, O Cornelius, * we bless thee * as a God-pleasing hierarch ** and an invincible martyr.`},{tone:4,spec_mel:`Thou hast given a sign`,text:`Following the character of thy goodness, * the foolish were shown to be wise; * and, having died and taken up thine abode in the grave * according to the law of nature, * thou makest it a well-spring of many wonders, * O wise Cornelius, * healing the sick * and dispelling evil spirits through the Holy Spirit, ** O divinely inspired one.`}],stichera_glory:{tone:6,text:`Celebrating the memory of the dedication, O Lord, and glorifying Thee, the Bestower of holiness, we pray: Let the senses of our souls be hallowed through the supplications of the glorious passion-bearers, O Good and almighty One.`},lic_theotokion:{tone:6,text:`Today hath the Tree appeared! Today hath the Jewish race perished! Today hath the Faith been made manifest by faithful sovereigns! Adam hath recovered from his fall because of the Tree, and again, through the Tree, have the demons come to tremble. O Lord Almighty, glory be to Thee!`},stichera_aposticha:[{tone:5,spec_mel:`Rejoice`,text:`Rejoice, O life-bearing Cross, invincible victory of piety, gate of paradise, confirmation of the faithful, rampart of the Church, whereby corruption hath been destroyed and abolished, and the power of death hath been trampled down, and we have been raised up from earth to the heavens, O invincible weapon, opponent of the demons, glory of the martyrs, true adornment of the venerable, haven of salvation, grant the world great mercy.`},{tone:5,spec_mel:`Rejoice`,verse:`Exalt ye the Lord our God, and worship the footstool of His feet; * for He is holy.`,text:`Rejoice, O Cross of the Lord, whereby mankind hath been released from the curse, thou ensign of joy, who in thine exaltation drivest away the enemy, O most honored one, our helper, dominion of kings, might of the righteous, splendor of priests, who, when traced, dost deliver from evils, staff of power, whereby we are shepherded, weapon of peace around whom the angels stand in fear, divine glory of Christ, Who granteth the world great mercy!`},{tone:5,spec_mel:`Rejoice`,verse:`God is our King before the ages, * He hath wrought salvation in the midst of the earth.`,text:`Rejoice, O guide of the blind, physician of the ailing, resurrection of all the dead, who hast lifted us up who have fallen into corruption, O precious Cross, whereby the curse hath been annulled and incorruption hath blossomed forth, and we mortals have been deified, and the devil hath been utterly cast down! Beholding thee today uplifted in the arms of the hierarch, we exalt Him Who was lifted up upon thee, and we bow down before thee, richly drawing forth great mercy.`}],aposticha_glory:{tone:2,text:`Celebrating the dedication of the most sacred temple of Thy resurrection, we glorify Thee, O Lord, Who hast sanctified it and perfected it with Thine all-perfect grace, Thou Who art adorned therein by the faithful with sanctifying, mystic and sacred sacrifices, receivest un-bloody and most pure sacrifices from the hands of Thy servants, bestowing great mercy and cleansing from sins upon those who rightly make offering.`},aposticha_both_now:{tone:2,text:`The Cross of the Bestower of life, a divine treasure hidden in the ground, was shown in the heavens to the pious emperor, noetically revealing an inscription of victory over the enemy. And joyfully hastening in godly manner with faith and love to see it on high, he zealously lifted it from the bosom of the earth, for the deliverance of the world and the salvation of our souls.`},troparion:{tone:4,text:`Thou didst show forth the beauty * of the holy dwelling-place of Thy glory here below, O Lord, * like the splendor of the firmament on high. * Establish it throughout all ages, * and through the Theotokos * accept the supplications we unceasingly offer unto Thee therein, ** O Thou life and resurrection of all.`},troparion_second:{tone:4,text:`As thou didst share in the ways of the apostles * and didst occupy their throne, * thou didst find thine activity to be a passage to divine vision, * O divinely inspired one. * Wherefore, ordering the word of truth, * thou didst suffer for the Faith even to the shedding of thy blood, ** O Hieromartyr Cornelius, entreat Christ God, that our souls be saved.`},troparion_bothnow:{tone:4,text:`O Lord, we offer unto Thee in supplication * the life-creating Cross of Thy goodness, * which Thou hast bestowed upon us, * unworthy though we are. * Save Thou all Orthodox Christians, and this Thy city, * which entreateth Thee through the Theotokos, ** O Thou Who alone lovest mankind.`},kontakion_ode3:{tone:4,spec_mel:`Thou hast appeared`,text:`The Church hath been shown to be a heaven of great splendor, * which enlighteneth all the faithful; ** and, standing therein, we cry aloud: Establish this house, O Lord!`},kontakion_ode6:{tone:2,spec_mel:`The steadfast`,text:`Grant inner enlightenment and spiritual renewal of heart * unto those who with faith celebrate * the sacred dedication of the temple of Thy house, * of which it was Thy good pleasure to have built in Thy divine name, ** O Thou Who art glorious in the saints.`},ikos:`The most wise Solomon of old, celebrating the memorial of the dedication, brought dumb beasts as whole-burnt offerings and sacrifices to God; and now that truth and grace have come to earth, He Who offered Himself as a sacrifice for our salvation hath abolished sacrifices, in that He is the Lover of mankind. He Who alone is glorious in the saints hath sanctified the church and shown it to be unshakable.`,matins_exapostilarion_feast:{spec_mel:`By the Spirit in the sanctuary`,text:`The most wondrous and most precious and holy Church, which Thou didst redeem by Thy most pure blood, O good Word, do Thou renew by the Spirit, and adorn it with divine rays, glorifying it by the glorying of those who properly celebrate the dedication of thy temple.`},exapostilarion:`With light-giving splendors, the Cross of the Lord summoneth to its elevation all who with divine love stand before it. Come ye all, and with joy, love, fear and faith let us kiss it and glorify the one Creator and Master!`,beatitudes_source:`menaion`,beatitudes_troparia:[{text:`O Christ, by the Spirit Thou hast sanctified Thy Church on earth, anointing it today with the oil of Thy gladness.`,source:`Ode III`,label:`Ode III, 1`,note:`(Twice) per PDF`},{text:`O good One, Thou hast revealed today the tabernacle of Thy glory, a well-built dwelling-place made by the hands of men, which surpasseth understanding.`,source:`Ode III`,label:`Ode III, 2`},{text:`Having Thee as an immovable foundation, O Christ, the Church is crowned with Thy Cross as with a royal diadem.`,source:`Ode III`,label:`Ode III, 3`},{text:`Christ the King desired the beauty of the Church He now hath chosen, and hath shown her to be the mother of the gentiles, who from slaves have been made sons by the Spirit.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI, 1`},{text:`The hordes of the malicious demons tremble before the Church of Christ which is marked with the sign of the image of the Cross and covered with the overshadowing of the Spirit.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI, 2`},{text:`Having Christ, and not sand, as its foundation, the Church of the nations is crowned with unapproachable beauty and adorned with a royal diadem.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI, 3`},{text:`O wonder more recent than all other wonders! Without knowing a man the Virgin hath conceived in her womb Him Who sustaineth all things, yet without confining Him.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI, Theotokion`}]},"09-14":{saint:`The Universal Exaltation of the Precious & Life-creating Cross`,oca_primary:!0,source_file:`09-14.pdf`,rank:`great_feast`,fekula_section:`2F`,has_great_doxology:!0,has_polyeleos:!0,has_litya:!0,has_paroemias:!0,magnificat_sung:!1,matins_format:`god_is_the_lord`,matins_gospel:`John 12:28-36 (§42)`,aposticha_source:`menaion`,magnification:`We magnify Thee, O Christ, Bestower of life, and we honor Thy holy Cross, whereby Thou hast saved us from slavery to the enemy.`,magnification_selected_psalm:`Judge them, O God, that do me injustice; war against them that war against me.`,note:"RANK — THE ONE PLACE THIS MONTH WHERE A PRINTED RANK WAS NOT TAKEN AT FACE VALUE, AND THE REASONING IS RECORDED HERE IN FULL. ODS 3rd ed. vol. III, Services of the Menaion, pp. 23-25 (`ods3-0914-any`) prints 'A vigil-rank service on any day', and encoding_rule_v2.md §1.1 step 0 says a printed rank is decisive. It is nonetheless encoded rank: 'great_feast' for three converging reasons. (1) Vigil-rank is the FLOOR for the Twelve Great Feasts, not a ceiling — every one of the Twelve receives at least an All-Night Vigil, so 'vigil-rank' states the service structure the feast requires and does not cap its festal class. (2) The codebase already settles the class elsewhere: FIXED_GREAT_FEASTS in hours-tool.jsx carries 09-14 as rank: 'great' with forLord: true, a Great Prokeimenon group, and the note 'One of the Twelve Great Feasts'. The `season` layer derives great_feast from the fixed calendar independently of any Menaion entry's rank, so class and service-structure are already separate axes in the code; §1.1 is the only place that collapses them. (3) Ranking this vigil while 09-08 (Nativity of the Theotokos) is great_feast would make the festal class of two of the Twelve depend on the accident of which dates vol. III happens to cover — vol. III has no 09-08 entry. Both feasts print AT LITTLE VESPERS, both carry Polyeleos, Litiya, three Lessons and the Great Doxology; treating them differently would encode an artifact of source coverage as a liturgical distinction. OPEN RULE DEFECT, FLAGGED NOT FIXED: §1.1 step 1's Great Feast list enumerates ELEVEN feasts and omits the Exaltation of the Cross, which is why step 1 cannot resolve this date on its own and step 0 appears to win by default. Nothing in the code reads that list, so this is a documentation gap rather than a live defect — but it is the reason the two steps appear to conflict here, and it should be corrected deliberately rather than as a side effect of this encoding. LIC: 8 slots from 3 unique texts, marked (Thrice), (Thrice), (Twice). Not a uniform multiple, so positional repeatIndex markers per §6b. BEATITUDES ARE ABSENT BY RUBRIC, NOT UNENCODED. The Liturgy opens with the three FESTAL ANTIPHONS, which replace the Beatitudes entirely on this feast. Per §0 this is an ABSENT field, not a gap. The antiphon verses and refrains are printed in the PDF; there is no field vocabulary for festal antiphons in V1, so they are described here rather than encoded — a real capture gap for a Great Feast, worth a schema decision. TRISAGION IS REPLACED: 'Instead of the Trisagion, we chant: We venerate Thy Cross, O Master, and we glorify Thy holy Resurrection!' Captured in trisagion_replacement. ZADOSTOINIK: 'It is truly meet' is replaced by the Ode IX irmos of Canon I with the festal refrain. This is also why magnificat_sung is false — the Magnificat is displaced by the refrain 'Magnify, O my soul, the all-honored Cross of the Lord!', which the PDF directs to be chanted before each of the six troparia of Canon I. SINGLE KONTAKION, verified at both the Ode VI position and AT LITURGY per §5.x; routes to all four Hours. OCR ARTIFACT, NOT A SOURCE DEFECT: the Liturgy Gospel heading extracts as 'ACCORDING TO JOHN, §60 (IN. 19: ...)' — capital I-N for JN. Corrected to John here. Distinct from the §1.3 errata class, which are defects in the printed book itself. SCOPE: §6 restricts full canon odes, sessional hymns and Praises stichera to Great Feasts requested BY DATE. No such request was made, so the canons, the sessional sets and the four Praises stichera are in the PDF and deliberately not captured.",feast_e:`First Corinthians 1:18-24 (§125)`,feast_g:`John 19:6-11, 13-20, 25-28, 30-35 (§60)`,prokeimenon_tone:7,prokeimenon_text:`Exalt ye the Lord our God, and worship the footstool of His feet, * for He is holy.`,prokeimenon_stichos:`The Lord is King, let the peoples rage.`,alleluia_tone:4,alleluia_verse:`Remember Thy congregation which Thou hast purchased from the beginning.`,alleluia_stichos:`God is our King before the ages, He hath wrought salvation in the midst of the earth.`,communion_verse:`The light of Thy countenance, O Lord, hath been signed upon us.`,matins_prokeimenon:`All the ends of the earth * have seen the salvation of our God. (Tone IV) Verse: O sing unto the Lord a new song, for the Lord hath wrought wondrous things.`,trisagion_replacement:`We venerate Thy Cross, O Master, * and we glorify Thy holy Resurrection!`,paroemia_1:`Exodus — Moses casts the tree into the bitter waters of Marah and they are made sweet (Exodus 15:22-16:1)`,paroemia_2:`Proverbs — despise not the chastening of the Lord; she is a tree of life to them that lay hold upon her (Proverbs 3:11-18)`,paroemia_3:`Isaiah — thy gates shall be opened continually, and the glory of Lebanon shall come to thee (Isaiah 60:11-16)`,stichera_lord_i_call_count:8,stichera_lord_i_call:[{tone:6,spec_mel:`Having set aside`,text:`Raised on high, * the Cross exhorteth all creation * to hymn the most pure Passion * of Him who was lifted up thereon. * For thereon having slain him that slew us, * in that He is merciful, * He gave life unto the dead, * and in the exceeding greatness of His goodness * He hath made them beautiful and deemed them worthy to live in the heavens. * Wherefore, rejoicing, * we exalt His name, ** and magnify His extreme condescension.`},{repeatIndex:0},{repeatIndex:0},{tone:6,spec_mel:`Having set aside`,text:`Moses prefigured Thee, * stretching forth his arms on high, * and vanquished the tyrant Amalek, * O precious Cross, boast of the faithful, * confirmation of sufferers, * savior of all the venerable. * Wherefore, creation, * beholding thee lifted up, is gladdened, * and keepeth festival, * glorifying Christ, * Who through thee hath gathered the dispersed ** in His extreme goodness.`},{repeatIndex:3},{repeatIndex:3},{tone:6,spec_mel:`Having set aside`,text:`O most precious Cross, * who art today exalted, * and about whom the ranks of angels stand in gladness, * by divine command thou dost uplift all * who for the stealing of the fruit * were driven out and were sunk in death. * Wherefore, we, the faithful, * venerating thee with hearts and lips, * derive from thee sanctification, * crying aloud: Exalt ye Christ, * the supremely good God, ** and worship His divine footstool.`},{repeatIndex:6}],stichera_glory:{tone:2,text:`Come, all ye nations, let us worship the blessed Tree whereby everlasting righteousness hath come to be; for he who by the tree deceived our forefather Adam, is himself deceived by the Cross, and he who by tyranny held fast the royal house, falleth, cast down by a strange fall. By the Blood of God the venom of the serpent is washed away, and the curse of the just condemnation was lifted by the Righteous One when He was condemned by an unjust sentence; for it was fitting that the tree be healed by a Tree, and that, by the sufferings of the Dispassionate One on the Tree, the sufferings of the condemned be loosed. Glory, O Christ our King, to Thy wise dispensation toward us, whereby Thou hast saved all, in that Thou art good and the Lover of mankind.`},lic_theotokion:null,stichera_aposticha:[{tone:5,spec_mel:`Rejoice`,text:`Rejoice, thou Life-bearing Cross, invincible victory of piety, door to paradise, confirmation of the faithful, rampart of the Church, whereby corruption hath been utterly destroyed, the dominion of death trampled down, and we have been lifted up from earth to those who are in heaven, invincible weapon, contender against the demons, glory of the martyrs, true adornment of the venerable, haven of salvation, which grantest the world great mercy.`},{tone:5,spec_mel:`Rejoice`,verse:`Exalt ye the Lord our God, and worship the footstool of His feet, * for He is holy.`,text:`Rejoice, thou Cross of the Lord, whereby mankind hath been loosed from the curse, all-honorable standard of joy which disperseth the enemy in thine exaltation, our helper, might of kings, Strength of the righteous, splendor of priests; which, when traced, doth deliver from misfortune, staff of power whereby we are tended, weapon of peace which the angels surround with fear, divine glory of Christ Who granteth the world great mercy.`},{tone:5,spec_mel:`Rejoice`,verse:`God is our King before the ages; * He hath wrought salvation in the midst of the earth.`,text:`Rejoice, thou guide of the blind, physician of the ailing, resurrection of all the dead, which hast raised us up who had fallen into corruption, O precious Cross, whereby the curse was annulled and incorruption budded forth, and the earthly have been deified, and the devil utterly cast down! Beholding thee raised on high today in the hands of the high priest, we exalt Him who was exalted in thy midst, and bow down before thee, plenteously drawing forth from thee great mercy.`}],aposticha_glory:{tone:8,text:`That which Moses prefigured with his body of old cast down and vanquished Amalek; and David the psalmist, crying out, hath commanded us to bow down before Thy footstool, Thy precious Cross, O Christ God. Today we sinners also do homage with our unworthy lips to Thee Whose will it was to be nailed thereto; and, chanting, we pray: O Lord, grant that with the thief we may receive Thy kingdom!`},aposticha_both_now:null,troparion:{tone:1,text:`Save O Lord Thy people, * and bless Thine inheritance. * Grant now unto the faithful * victory over adversaries, * and by the power of Thy Cross ** do Thou preserve Thy commonwealth.`},kontakion_ode6:{tone:4,text:`O Thou Who wast lifted up willingly upon the Cross, * bestow Thy mercies upon the new community named after Thee, * O Christ God; gladden with Thy power Orthodox Christians, * granting them victory over enemies; ** may they have as Thy help the invincible trophy, the weapon of peace.`},ikos:`He who was caught up to the third heaven of paradise and heard unspeakable and divine words which the human tongue cannot utter, what writeth he to the Galatians, which, as lovers of the Scriptures, ye have both read and come to understand? God forbid, saith he, that I should glory, save only in the Cross of the Lord, whereon having suffered He slew the passions. Let us all then firmly hold this boast, the Cross of the Lord; for this Wood is our salvation, the invincible trophy, the weapon of peace.`,matins_exapostilarion_feast:{spec_mel:`When the disciples`,text:`The Cross is the guardian of the whole world! The Cross is the beauty of the Church! The Cross is the dominion of kings! The Cross is the confirmation of the faithful! The Cross is the glory of the angels and the wounding of the demons!`},matins_exapostilarion_theotokion:{spec_mel:`Hearken, ye women`,text:`Today is the Cross raised on high, and the world is sanctified; for He that sitteth with the Father and the Holy Spirit stretched out his arms thereon. It hath brought the whole world to the knowledge of Thee, O Christ. Therefore, to those who hope in Thee do Thou grant divine glory.`},it_is_truly_meet_suppressed:!0,zadostoinik_refrain:`Magnify, O my soul, the all-honored Cross of the Lord!`,zadostoinik_irmos:`O Theotokos, thou art a mystical Paradise, * who untilled brought forth Christ. * by Whom the life-giving Tree of the Cross hath been planted upon the earth: * Therefore celebrating its exaltation on this day, * Him do we worship * and thee do we magnify.`},"09-19":[{saint:`Afterfeast of the Exaltation of the Precious Cross; Commemoration of the Holy Martyrs Trophimus, Sabbatius & Dorymedon`,oca_primary:!0,source_file:`09-19.pdf`,rank:`six_stichera`,fekula_section:`2G1`,fekula_section_override:`2G1`,has_great_doxology:!1,has_polyeleos:!1,has_litya:!1,has_paroemias:!1,magnificat_sung:!1,matins_format:`god_is_the_lord`,aposticha_source:`menaion`,it_is_truly_meet_suppressed:!0,note:"RANK PER encoding_rule_v2.md §1.1 WATERFALL, RUN IN FULL because ODS 3rd ed. vol. III does not cover 19 September (no `ods3-0919` entry), so §1.1 step 0 does not apply. Step 1 — not a Great Feast: this is the AFTERFEAST of the Exaltation, and an afterfeast day is not itself a member of the closed enumerated list of Twelve Great Feasts (14 September is); the Cross material here is afterfeast hymnography layered under a saint. Step 2 — not Vigil: no Small Vespers in 09-19.pdf. Step 3 — not Polyeleos: Matins appoints no Polyeleos, no Magnification, no Matins Gospel. Step 4 — not Doxology: no Great Doxology is appointed, and the PDF prints a Matins Aposticha, which the Great Doxology would displace. Step 5 — Six-Stichera: 'On “Lord, I have cried ...,” 6 Stichera: 3 of the feast, in Tone IV ... And 3 Stichera of the holy martyrs, in Tone VIII'. Feast-plus-saint split → Fekula §2G1, set both as fekula_section and as fekula_section_override. APOSTICHA SOURCE IS MENAION, printed verbatim: 'On the Aposticha, these Stichera of the feast, in Tone I'. Encoded with their psalm verses on the stichera they precede (the first sticheron carries no verse). KONTAKION ROUTING (§5): the PDF prints the martyrs' kontakion (Tone VIII, Spec. Mel. “As the first-fruits ...”) after ODE III and the kontakion of the feast/Cross (Tone IV, 'O Thou Who wast lifted up willingly upon the Cross') after ODE VI. Stored as kontakion_ode3 = holy martyrs → 1st & 6th Hours, kontakion_ode6 = feast → 3rd & 9th Hours. The AT LITURGY block reprints both in the same order. ONE IKOS ONLY, AND IT BELONGS TO THE CROSS. The PDF prints an ikos immediately after the Ode VI kontakion of the feast ('He that was caught up to the third heaven of paradise ...' — the Galatians/boasting-in-the-Cross ikos), so it pairs with kontakion_ode6 and is stored in `ikos`, which menaion-browser.jsx labels 'Ikos (after Ode VI)'. NO ikos is printed for the martyrs after Ode III, so `ikos_ode3` is absent by the source, not unencoded. MAJOR ABSENCE — NO LITURGY PROPERS PRINTED. The AT LITURGY block of 09-19.pdf prints ONLY: the Beatitudes, the two troparia, the two kontakia, the 'Instead of “It is truly meet ...”' zadostoinik, and the Communion Verse. It prints NO Prokeimenon, NO Alleluia, NO Epistle and NO Gospel — the Epistle/Gospel pericopes and the prokeimenon/alleluia that 09-07 and 09-20 both print are simply not in this file. feast_e, feast_g, prokeimenon_* and alleluia_* are therefore ABSENT BY THE SOURCE, not skipped; prokeimenon_note records the absence explicitly so it cannot be mistaken for an encoding gap. The likely rubrical reason is that within the afterfeast the readings of the day (from the continuous cycle) are used, but the PDF does not say so and that inference is NOT encoded as fact. ZADOSTOINIK CAPTURED: 'Instead of “It is truly meet ...,” we chant the Irmos of ODE IX of Canon I', with the refrain 'Magnify, O my soul, the all-honored Cross of the Lord!'. Encoded as it_is_truly_meet_suppressed / instead_of_it_is_truly_meet_refrain / instead_of_it_is_truly_meet_irmos. This is afterfeast behavior and is the strongest internal confirmation that the Cross layer is genuinely live on this date. COMBINED GLORY/BOTH-NOW, TWICE. Both at Lord I Have Cried ('Glory ..., Both now ..., in Tone IV') and at the Aposticha ('Glory ..., Both now ..., in Tone V') the PDF prints a SINGLE hymn serving both slots. Each is stored once, in stichera_glory and aposticha_glory; the Both-now partner fields are omitted rather than duplicated, per the 09-06 precedent. Note that neither doxasticon is of the martyrs — both are of the Cross, which is itself evidence that the afterfeast, not the saints, carries the doxastika at this rank. EXAPOSTILARION: only ONE is printed, 'Exapostilarion of the feast', followed by 'Glory ..., Both now ..., the foregoing is repeated'. matins_exapostilarion_theotokion is therefore absent by rubric — there is no second text — and the repeat is recorded here rather than encoded as a duplicate field. TROPARION ORDER: Vespers prints the martyrs' troparion first, then 'Glory ..., Both now ..., Troparion of the feast, in Tone I'; `troparion` = martyrs, `troparion_second` = feast. At 'God is the Lord' the order inverts (feast Twice, Glory martyrs, Both now feast Once). menaion-browser.jsx does not currently render `troparion_second`. BEATITUDES: 'On the Beatitudes, 6 Troparia from ODE VII of the canon of the feast.' THREE unique texts, EACH marked (Twice) — a uniform 3→6 doubling. Encoded as three entries each carrying the (Twice) note, with beatitudes_count 6. All six slots come from the FEAST canon; the martyrs contribute nothing to the Beatitudes on this date. SAME-DATE ALTERNATE SERVICE EXISTS AND IS NOT THIS ENTRY. A second extraction, 09-19A, prints a DIFFERENT and higher-ranked 19 September service: 'COMMEMORATION OF THE HOLY PASSION-BEARER, THE PIOUS PRINCE IGOR OF CHERNIGOV', AT GREAT VESPERS, with 'Blessed is the man ...' and 8 Stichera at Lord I Have Cried (3 of the feast in Tone VI + 5 of the passion-bearer in Tone II, two of them marked (Twice)). That is a separate Slavic commemoration at a different rank, NOT an alternate printing of this service, and it is deliberately not merged here. Flagged for whoever assembles September. OCA CHECK — NO OVERRIDE APPLIED. The PDF's commemoration matches the commemoration OCA lists as primary for 19 September (Martyrs Trophimus, Sabbatius and Dorymedon), so oca_primary is true. No live OCA lookup was possible in this pass (no network), so no hymn-by-hymn comparison was made and no §2.1 step 5 override was applied; the St. Sergius thou/thy text governs per §11 #17. This matters more than usual here because OCA does publish Liturgy readings for this date while the PDF prints none — but importing them unverified would be inventing data, so the absence stands. SCOPE: full canon odes (both canons), the two Sessional Hymns after the chantings of the Psalter, the Sessional Hymns after Ode III, and the Matins Aposticha (Tone VI, Spec. Mel. “On the third day ...”) are out of scope at this rank per §6 and are deliberately not captured.",prokeimenon_note:`NOT IN PDF — the AT LITURGY block of 09-19.pdf prints no Prokeimenon, no Alleluia, and no Epistle or Gospel pericope; only Beatitudes, troparia, kontakia, the zadostoinik and the Communion Verse. Absent by the source.`,communion_verse:`The light of Thy countenance, O Lord, hath been signed upon us.`,stichera_lord_i_call_count:6,stichera_lord_i_call:[{tone:4,spec_mel:`Thou hast given a sign`,text:`The all-holy Cross, * the invincible trophy, * cometh forth today, revealed from the earth * like a hidden treasure, * enriching the whole world * with the rays of its universal goodness. * Wherefore, we glorify the supremely good economy * of Christ our God ** Who deigned to be crucified thereon.`},{tone:4,spec_mel:`Thou hast given a sign`,text:`Of old, the Cross, depicted * by Moses, the faithful beholder and favorite of God, * the divine prophet, * routed the hordes of the aliens; * and now, depicted in the divine elevation * at the hands of the sacred ministers, * it dispelleth the hordes of the demons * and the assemblies of the Jews, ** and is the reproof of the audacity of the ungodly.`},{tone:4,spec_mel:`Thou hast given a sign`,text:`Thy Cross, O our Savior, * is the invincible might of Christians, * whereby multitudes of alien nations * are vanquished, * and the peace of Thy Church, O Christ * is bestowed upon those who render goodly glory. * Venerating it now, * we entreat Thee, O Lover of mankind: ** Deem us worthy of a portion with Thy saints.`},{tone:8,spec_mel:`Thy martyrs`,text:`Desiring to take pleasure in inexhaustible food, O martyr Trophimus, thou didst strive with the pangs of thy body and didst pass over from corruption to incorruption, rejoicing and adorned with thy martyric witness. Wherefore, by thy supplications entreat great mercy for all.`},{tone:8,spec_mel:`Thy martyrs`,text:`The glorious Sabbatius, having endured many torments, hath now been deemed worthy to celebrate with great joy in the courts of heaven. Receiving honors for his struggles, he is numbered among the assemblies of the angels. By his supplications, O Christ, grant Thy people great mercy.`},{tone:8,spec_mel:`Thy martyrs`,text:`Setting at naught the counsel of the ungodly, thou didst make thy mind steadfast with divine counsels, O Dorymedon, manfully entering the trials of tortures, and receiving trophies of honor. And thou reignest ever with Christ, praying for us all.`}],stichera_glory:{tone:4,text:`Let us clap our hands today for the victory which is praised with hymns, and with a radiant countenance and tongue let us openly cry: O Christ, Who for our sake didst deign to be tried, to be spat upon and scourged and arrayed in a purple robe, and Who ascended the Cross; Whom beholding, the sun and the moon hid their light, and the earth trembled with fear, and the veil of the temple was rent in twain: Do Thou now grant unto us Thine honored Cross as a preserver and protector, and a dispeller of demons; that, kissing it, we may all cry aloud to it: Save us by thy power, O Cross! Sanctify us by Thy radiance, O precious Cross! And fortify us by thine exaltation, for thou hast been given to us as the light and salvation of our souls.`},stichera_aposticha:[{tone:1,spec_mel:`Joy of the ranks of heaven`,text:`The Cross is raised up, and the demons are driven away; * the thief openeth the gates of Eden; * death is slain, and now is shown to be void; * and Christ is magnified. * Wherefore, all mortals are glad, ** for the curse hath been annulled.`},{tone:1,spec_mel:`Joy of the ranks of heaven`,verse:`Exalt ye the Lord our God, and worship the footstool of His feet, * for He is holy.`,text:`Come, all ye who love God, * and, beholding the precious Cross uplifted, * let us magnify together, and give glory * to the one Redeemer and God, crying aloud: * O Thou Who wast crucified on the tree of the Cross, ** disdain not those who entreat Thee.`},{tone:1,spec_mel:`Joy of the ranks of heaven`,verse:`God is our King before the ages; * He hath wrought salvation in the midst of the earth.`,text:`Moses, of old, sweetening bitterness, delivered Israel, * prefigured the Cross in image; * and all of us, the faithful, * depicting it ever in our hearts * in godly manner and mystically, ** are saved by its might.`}],aposticha_glory:{tone:5,text:`The words of Thy prophets, Isaiah and David, have been fulfilled, O God, which said: All nations will come, O Lord, and bow down before Thee. For, behold the people who have been filled with Thy grace in Thy courts in Jerusalem, O good One. O Thou Who didst endure the Cross for us and hast imparted life through Thy resurrection, preserve and save us!`},troparion:{tone:8,text:`God Who is praised in the Trinity hath glorified a trinity of martyrs: * Trophimus, Sabbatius and Dorymedon. * For, believing in Him, they cast down the enemy. ** By their supplications, O Christ our God, have mercy upon us.`},troparion_second:{tone:1,text:`Save O Lord Thy people, * and bless Thine inheritance. * Grant now unto the faithful * victory over adversaries, * and by the power of Thy Cross ** do Thou preserve Thy commonwealth.`},kontakion_ode3:{tone:8,spec_mel:`As the first-fruits`,text:`As the foundation of spiritual athletes and confirmation of piety * the Church doth honor and glorify thy luminous suffering, * O right wise and glorious Trophimus, ever-hymned and blessed spiritual athlete. * With those who suffered with thee ask cleansing for those who hymn thee, ** in that thou art invincible.`},kontakion_ode6:{tone:4,text:`O Thou Who wast lifted up willingly upon the Cross, * bestow Thy mercies upon the new community named after Thee, * O Christ God; gladden with Thy power Orthodox Christians, * granting them victory over enemies; ** may they have as Thy help the invincible trophy, the weapon of peace.`},ikos:`He that was caught up to the third heaven of paradise and heard unspeakable and divine words which the human tongue cannot utter, what writeth he to the Galatians, which, as lovers of the Scriptures, ye have both read and come to understand? God forbid, saith he, that I should glory, save only in the Cross of the Lord, whereon having suffered He slew the passions. Let us all then firmly hold this boast, the Cross of the Lord; for this Wood is our salvation, the invincible trophy, the weapon of peace.`,exapostilarion:`The Cross is the preservation of the whole world; the Cross is the adornment of the Church; the Cross is the might of kings; the Cross is the confirmation of the faithful; the Cross is the glory of angels and the wounding of demons.`,beatitudes_source:`menaion`,beatitudes_count:6,beatitudes_troparia:[{text:`The first man, tasting of the tree, went to dwell in corruption; for, having condemned himself to a shameful banishment from life, he imparted corruption to the whole race of mankind. But we mortals, finding restoration through the tree of the Cross, cry aloud: O supremely hymned God of our fathers, blessed art Thou!`,source:`Ode VII`,label:`Ode VII, 1`,note:`(Twice) per PDF`},{text:`Breaking the commandment of God came about through disobedience, and the untimely partaking of the fruit of the tree brought death to mankind, but to preserve that which is most precious, access to the tree of life was forbidden, until God opened it to the suffering thief who with a proper understanding cried aloud: O supremely hymned God of our fathers, blessed art Thou!`,source:`Ode VII`,label:`Ode VII, 2`,note:`(Twice) per PDF`},{text:`Israel, foreseeing things to come, laid hold of the tip of Joseph’s staff, revealing beforehand that the most glorious Cross would seize the power of the kingdom, for it is the victorious boast of kings and a light for those who cry aloud with faith: O supremely hymned God of our fathers, blessed art Thou!`,source:`Ode VII`,label:`Ode VII, 3`,note:`(Twice) per PDF`}],instead_of_it_is_truly_meet_refrain:`Magnify, O my soul, the all-honored Cross of the Lord!`,instead_of_it_is_truly_meet_irmos:`O Theotokos, thou art a mystical Paradise, * who untilled brought forth Christ. * by Whom the life-giving Tree of the Cross hath been planted upon the earth: * Therefore celebrating its exaltation on this day, * Him do we worship * and thee do we magnify.`},{saint:`Holy Passion-bearer, the Pious Prince Igor of Chernigov, Who Received the Name George in Monastic Tonsure`,oca_primary:!1,source_file:`09-19A.pdf`,rank:`polyeleos`,fekula_section:`2E`,has_great_doxology:!0,has_polyeleos:!0,has_litya:!1,has_paroemias:!0,magnificat_sung:!0,matins_format:`god_is_the_lord`,matins_gospel:`Matthew 10:16-22 (§36)`,aposticha_source:`menaion`,magnification:`We magnify thee, O holy passion-bearer Prince Igor, and we reverence thine honored sufferings, which thou didst endure for Christ.`,magnification_selected_psalm:`Our God is refuge and strength. A helper in afflictions which mightily befall us.`,matins_prokeimenon:`The righteous man shall flourish like a palm-tree, * and like a cedar in Lebanon shall he be multiplied. (Tone IV) Verse: They that are planted in the house of the Lord, in the courts of our God they shall blossom forth.`,note:"SECOND COMMEMORATION, NOT THE PRIMARY. This entry is the Prince Igor service printed in 09-19A.pdf and is an array element behind the day's primary commemoration. `oca_primary` is false: the OCA calendar for 19 September does NOT list Prince Igor of Chernigov — it lists the Martyrs Trophimus, Sabbatius and Dorymedon, the Right-believing Prince Theodore of Smolensk and Yaroslavl with his sons David and Constantine, Venerable Zosimas, and Theodore of Tarsus. Igor is a Russian/ROCOR commemoration absent from the OCA calendar, and per encoding_rule_v2.md §1 OCA primacy the OCA calendar governs which commemoration is primary, so this one is recorded as secondary. No OCA text exists for him, so no OCA override was available or applied and no OCA/St. Sergius divergence could be checked. NOTE the near-collision: OCA's Prince Theodore of Smolensk is a DIFFERENT right-believing prince from Igor of Chernigov; they are not two versions of one saint. RANK PER §1.1 WATERFALL, RUN IN FULL because ODS 3rd ed. vol. III does not cover this commemoration (no `ods3-0919A` entry among the 77), so §1.1 step 0 does not apply. Step 1 Great Feast — NO: Igor is not on the closed enumerated list. 19 September falls inside the afterfeast of the Exaltation of the Cross (14-21 September) and the PDF combines Igor's propers with 'the feast' throughout, but an afterfeast day is not itself a Great Feast and rank is a property of Igor's own commemoration. Step 2 Vigil — NO: 09-19A.pdf prints no Small Vespers; the file opens directly at 'AT GREAT VESPERS'. Step 3 Polyeleos — YES: p.5 prints 'Polyeleos, and this magnification: We magnify thee, O holy passion-bearer Prince Igor, and we reverence thine honored sufferings, which thou didst endure for Christ.', followed by Selected Psalm verses and a post-Polyeleos sessional hymn, and no Small Vespers precedes it. The waterfall stops here; steps 4-6 were NOT reached. For the record the Great Doxology is also appointed (p.15, 'Great Doxology, Troparia, Litanies, First Hour.') and six stichera are sung on the Praises — both consistent with polyeleos rank, and neither is diagnostic once step 3 has fired. KONTAKION ROUTING: the PDF prints TWO DISTINCT kontakia. At the Ode III position (p.7) 'Kontakion of the Cross, in Tone IV' ('O Thou Who wast lifted up willingly upon the Cross...') → `kontakion_ode3`, which §5 routes to the 1st and 6th Hours. At the Ode VI position (p.9) 'Kontakion of the holy passion-bearer, in Tone VI' ('Thou didst exchange the glory of an earthly princedom...') → `kontakion_ode6`, routed to the 3rd and 9th Hours. Igor's Ikos ('Thy princely diadem was stained with thy blood...') is printed immediately after the Ode VI kontakion and is stored in `ikos`, which menaion-browser.jsx labels 'Ikos (after Ode VI)' — truthful here. Both kontakia are printed AGAIN under AT LITURGY (p.17), Igor's first and the feast's second; that is a reprint of the same two texts, not a third and fourth kontakion. LORD I HAVE CRIED: 8 slots from 6 unique texts — 3 of the feast (the Exaltation, Tone VI, slots 0-2, one each) and 5 of the passion-bearer (Tone II, Spec. Mel. 'When from the Tree', slots 3-7 from 3 unique texts). The PDF marks the first TWO of Igor's stichera '(Twice)' and the third once, so the slot count 8 is not an integral multiple of the 6 unique texts; per §6b positional `{ repeatIndex: N }` markers with no text field are used at slots 4 and 6 rather than uniform doubling. LORD I HAVE CRIED BOTH-NOW IS NOT A THEOTOKION. The PDF prints 'Glory ..., of the holy passion-bearer, in Tone VI' and then 'Both now ..., of the feast, in Tone II' — the Both-now slot carries a sticheron OF THE CROSS ('Come, all ye nations, let us worship the blessed Tree...'), not a theotokion. It is therefore stored in `stichera_both_now`, not in `lic_theotokion`; putting a Cross sticheron under the browser's 'Both now (Theotokion)' label would be false on screen. KNOWN DISPLAY GAP: menaion-browser.jsx does not currently render `stichera_both_now`, so this text is in the data but not on screen — recorded rather than worked around, exactly as 09-05 records the `ikos_ode3` display gap. The Vespers Aposticha Both-now is likewise 'of the feast, in Tone VIII' and is stored in `aposticha_both_now`, which IS rendered. MATINS EXAPOSTILARION: the Glory/Both-now exapostilarion is printed as 'Exapostilarion of the feast' ('The Cross is the guardian of the whole world!...') — again of the Cross, not a theotokion — so it is stored in `matins_exapostilarion_feast` rather than in `matins_exapostilarion_theotokion`, for the same truthfulness reason. The PDF prints no tone for either exapostilarion. TROPARIA: Igor's own troparion (Tone IV) is in `troparion`. The feast's troparion of the Cross (Tone I, 'Save O Lord Thy people') occupies the 'Glory ..., Both now ...' slot at Vespers and the 'Both now' slot at 'God is the Lord', so it is stored in `troparion_bothnow`. TWO PROKEIMENA, TWO ALLELUIA VERSES, TWO COMMUNION VERSES AT LITURGY. The PDF prints the feast's prokeimenon first (Tone VII, 'Exalt ye the Lord our God...', marked 'also until the Leave-taking') and the saint's second (Tone VII, 'The righteous man shall be glad in the Lord...'), with a single Verse ('Hearken, O God, unto my prayer...') that belongs to the SAINT's prokeimenon. Because `prokeimenon_stichos` has no `prokeimenon_2_stichos` counterpart in the field vocabulary, the SAINT's prokeimenon is stored in the primary `prokeimenon_*` fields so that its stichos stays correctly paired, and the FEAST's is stored in `prokeimenon_2_*`; this inverts the printed order and `prokeimenon_note` says so. The Alleluia is handled the same way: the saint's (Tone IV, with its Verse) in the primary `alleluia_*` fields, the feast's (Tone IV, 'Remember Thy congregation which Thou hast purchased from the beginning', printed with no verse) in `alleluia_2_*`. Two communion verses are printed and there is only one `communion_verse` field, so both are stored in it with their printed labels kept inline. ZADOSTOINIK PRESENT BUT NOT CAPTURED: p.19 appoints, instead of 'It is truly meet', the Irmos of Ode IX of Canon I with the refrain 'Magnify, O my soul, the all-honored Cross of the Lord!' — an afterfeast-of-the-Exaltation proper. It is outside the field set for this encoding pass and is recorded here rather than dropped silently. MAGNIFICAT — FLAGGED, NOT RESOLVED. `magnificat_sung` is true because the PDF prints no rubric suppressing the Magnificat at Matins Ode IX and a polyeleos-rank service ordinarily sings it. The only refrain-substitution the PDF prints is the LITURGY zadostoinik above, which is a different element. That 19 September lies inside the Exaltation afterfeast raises the question of whether the Magnificat is displaced at Matins as well; the PDF does not say, so it is flagged here rather than inferred. PAROEMIA CITATIONS ABSENT FROM THE PDF: all three headings read 'A READING FROM THE PROPHECY OF ISAIAH' / 'A READING FROM THE WISDOM OF SOLOMON' (twice) with no chapter and verse. The passages are identified here from their printed text — the standard martyr/righteous set — and each field says the identification is not from the source. SCOPE: both canons at Matins (the feast's with 6 troparia and Igor's with 8), the sessional hymns, the Psalm 50 sticheron and the six Praises stichera are printed in the PDF and are deliberately NOT captured, per §6 and the encoding scope for ranks below Great Feast.",feast_e:`Acts of the Apostles 6:8-15; 7:1-5, 47-60 (§17)`,feast_g:`John 15:17-16:2 (§52)`,prokeimenon_tone:7,prokeimenon_text:`The righteous man shall be glad in the Lord, * and shall hope in Him.`,prokeimenon_stichos:`Hearken, O God, unto my prayer, when I make supplication unto Thee.`,prokeimenon_2_tone:7,prokeimenon_2_text:`Exalt ye the Lord our God, and worship the footstool of His feet; * for He is holy.`,prokeimenon_note:`The PDF prints the FEAST's prokeimenon first — "Prokeimenon of the feast in Tone VII (also until the Leave-taking)" — and the SAINT's second. The single printed Verse belongs to the saint's prokeimenon, so the saint's is stored in the primary prokeimenon_* fields (keeping stichos and text paired) and the feast's in prokeimenon_2_*; this inverts the printed order.`,alleluia_tone:4,alleluia_verse:`The righteous man shall flourish like a palm-tree, and like a cedar in Lebanon shall he be multiplied.`,alleluia_stichos:`They that are planted in the house of the Lord, in the courts of our God they shall blossom forth.`,alleluia_2_tone:4,alleluia_2_verse:`Remember Thy congregation which Thou hast purchased from the beginning`,communion_verse:`Of the feast: The light of Thy countenance, O Lord, hath been signed upon us. — Of the saint: In everlasting remembrance shall the righteous be; he shall not be afraid of evil tidings.`,paroemia_1:`Isaiah — "Be ye My witnesses, and I too am a witness, saith the Lord God" (Isaiah 43:9-14; identified from the printed text — the PDF heading reads only "A READING FROM THE PROPHECY OF ISAIAH", with no chapter and verse)`,paroemia_2:`Wisdom of Solomon — "The souls of the righteous are in the hand of God" (Wisdom of Solomon 3:1-9; identified from the printed text — the PDF heading reads only "A READING FROM THE WISDOM OF SOLOMON", with no chapter and verse)`,paroemia_3:`Wisdom of Solomon — "Though the righteous be prevented with death, yet shall he be in rest" (Wisdom of Solomon 4:7-15; identified from the printed text — the PDF heading reads only "A READING FROM THE WISDOM OF SOLOMON", with no chapter and verse)`,stichera_lord_i_call_count:8,stichera_lord_i_call:[{tone:6,text:`Raised on high, * the Cross exhorteth all creation * to hymn the most pure Passion * of Him who was lifted up thereon. * For thereon having slain him that slew us, * in that He is merciful, * He gave life unto the dead, * and in the exceeding greatness of His goodness * He hath made them beautiful and deemed them worthy to live in the heavens. * Wherefore, rejoicing, * we exalt His name, ** and magnify His extreme condescension.`},{tone:6,text:`Moses prefigured Thee, * stretching forth his arms on high, * and vanquished the tyrant Amalek, * O precious Cross, boast of the faithful, * confirmation of sufferers, * savior of all the venerable. * Wherefore, creation, * beholding thee lifted up, is gladdened, * and keepeth festival, glorifying Christ, * Who through thee hath gathered the dispersed ** in His extreme goodness.`},{tone:6,text:`O most precious Cross, * who art today exalted, * and about whom the ranks of angels stand in gladness, * by divine command thou dost uplift all * who for the stealing of the fruit were driven out and were sunk in death. * Wherefore, we, the faithful, * venerating thee with hearts and lips, * derive from thee sanctification, * crying aloud: Exalt ye Christ, the supremely good God, ** and worship His divine footstool.`},{tone:2,spec_mel:`When from the Tree`,text:`When of thine own will * thou didst forsake thine earthly kingdom, * and having desired to labor for the Lord in monasticism, * the enemy of the human race * inflicted many and cruel tribulations upon thee; * yet, firm as adamantine, thou wast not shaken thereby, * but manfully endure them all. * And now thou standest before Christ, ** praying for those who honor thy memory.`},{repeatIndex:3},{tone:2,spec_mel:`When from the Tree`,text:`When thou didst commit thyself * to the Monastery of Saint Theodore, * and there gave thyself over to monastic obedience, * the enemy of the human race incited lawless people against thee, * who unjustly dragged thee forth * to slaughter thee like an innocent lamb, * though thou hadst done no wrong to anyone. * But thou didst pray unto the Lord, * emulating the first martyr of Christ, and crying out with him: ** “O Lord, lay not this sin to their charge!”`},{repeatIndex:5},{tone:2,spec_mel:`When from the Tree`,text:`When thou wast praying on bended knees * before the image of the most pure one, * thy murderers, like wild beasts, * fell upon thee with rage; * and willingly giving thyself over * into their hands without complaint, * thou didst accept great suffering * and torment from them with patience, ** and didst thereby commit thy righteous soul to the Lord.`}],stichera_glory:{tone:6,text:`The city of Chernigov, which preserveth thy holy and much-suffering body in the Church of the Savior, rejoiceth today and crieth out to thee with love: O passion-bearer Prince Igor, entreat Christ God, that He preserve our land in peace profound, and that He protect this city and save us all.`},stichera_both_now:{tone:2,text:`Come, all ye nations, let us worship the blessed Tree whereby everlasting righteousness hath come to be; for he who by the tree deceived our forefather Adam, is himself deceived by the Cross, and he who by tyranny held fast the royal house, falleth, cast down by a strange fall. By the Blood of God the venom of the serpent is washed away, and the curse of the just condemnation was lifted by the Righteous One when He was condemned by an unjust sentence; for it was fitting that the tree be healed by a Tree, and that, by the sufferings of the Dispassionate One on the Tree, the sufferings of the condemned be loosed. Glory, O Christ our King, to Thy wise dispensation toward us, whereby Thou hast saved all, in that Thou art good and the Lover of mankind.`},stichera_aposticha:[{tone:2,spec_mel:`O house of Ephratha`,text:`Blessed art thou, O city of Kiev, * and holy are thy streets * which drank the precious blood * of the passion-bearing Prince Igor, ** who there was unjustly slain.`},{tone:2,spec_mel:`O house of Ephratha`,verse:`The righteous man shall flourish like a palm-tree, * and like a cedar in Lebanon shall he be multiplied.`,text:`Rejoice, O city of Chernigov! * Be glad, O holy house of the Savior! * For ye guard a priceless treasure: * the holy relics of the passion-bearer Igor, ** who prayeth unceasingly on behalf of our souls.`},{tone:2,spec_mel:`O house of Ephratha`,verse:`They that are planted in the house of the Lord, * in the courts of our God they shall blossom forth.`,text:`Suffering steadfastly * and manfully enduring tribulations, * thou didst behold the Lord face to face, * Who adorneth thee with the crown of martyrdom ** and hath caused thee to dwell in the mansions of heaven.`}],aposticha_glory:{tone:6,text:`Come, all ye who love the martyrs, let us glorify the glorious Igor, the passion-bearer of Christ; for he hath crushed the head of the serpent, and with his blood hath sanctified the earth, and having passed over from that which is on earth to the eternal habitations, he hath received honors for his struggles from the hand of the Almighty; and asketh that cleansing and great mercy be granted to our souls.`},aposticha_both_now:{tone:8,text:`That which Moses prefigured with his body of old cast down and vanquished Amalek; and David the psalmist, crying out, hath commanded us to bow down before Thy footstool, Thy precious Cross, O Christ God. Today we sinners also do homage with our unworthy lips to Thee Whose will it was to be nailed thereto; and, chanting, we pray: O Lord, grant that with the thief we may receive Thy kingdom!`},troparion:{tone:4,text:`Illumined by divine baptism, * enlightened by the splendors of the Holy Spirit, * thou didst take the Gospel of Christ into thy heart, * fulfilling the word of the Son of God by thy deeds, * O right-believing Prince Igor. * Entreat our supremely good Savior, * that He grant us peace and mercy, * and the salvation of the souls of us ** who cherish thine honored memory.`},troparion_bothnow:{tone:1,text:`Save O Lord Thy people, * and bless Thine inheritance. * Grant now unto the faithful * victory over adversaries, * and by the power of Thy Cross ** do Thou preserve Thy commonwealth.`},kontakion_ode3:{tone:4,text:`O Thou Who wast lifted up willingly upon the Cross, * bestow Thy mercies upon the new community named after Thee, * O Christ God; gladden with Thy power Orthodox Christians, * granting them victory over enemies; ** may they have as Thy help the invincible trophy, the weapon of peace.`},kontakion_ode6:{tone:6,text:`Thou didst exchange the glory of an earthly princedom * for the humble habit of a monk, * and, ending thine earthly life in martyrdom, * thou now rejoiceth in the heavens, * earnestly praying for those who honor thee, O Igor, ** boast of spiritual athletes.`},ikos:`Thy princely diadem was stained with thy blood, O divinely wise passion-bearer Igor; but taking the Cross in thy hand instead of a scepter, thou wast shown to be a victor. And didst offer thyself to the Master as an unblemished sacrifice; for like an innocent lamb thou wast slain by thine own subjects. And now, rejoicing, thou standest before the Holy Trinity. Pray thou that our souls be saved.`,exapostilarion:`Like the radiant sun hath thy memorial now shone forth, O passion-bearer, filling all the faithful with gladness; and they cry out to thee in supplication: Pray thou earnestly, that we be saved.`,matins_exapostilarion_feast:{text:`The Cross is the guardian of the whole world! The Cross is the beauty of the Church! The Cross is the dominion of kings! The Cross is the confirmation of the faithful! The Cross is the glory of the angels and the wounding of the demons!`},beatitudes_source:`menaion`,beatitudes_troparia:[{text:`The first man, tasting of the tree, went to dwell in corruption; for, having condemned himself to a shameful banishment from life, he imparted corruption to the whole race of mankind. But we mortals, finding restoration through the tree of the Cross, cry aloud: O supremely hymned God of our fathers, blessed art Thou!`,source:`Ode VII`,label:`Ode VII (feast), 1`,note:`(Twice) per PDF`},{text:`Breaking the commandment of God came about through disobedience, and the untimely partaking of the fruit of the tree brought death to mankind, but to preserve that which is most precious, access to the tree of life was forbidden, until God opened it to the suffering thief who with a proper understanding cried aloud: O supremely hymned God of our fathers, blessed art Thou!`,source:`Ode VII`,label:`Ode VII (feast), 2`},{text:`Israel, foreseeing things to come, laid hold of the tip of Joseph’s staff, revealing beforehand that the most glorious Cross would seize the power of the kingdom, for it is the victorious boast of kings and a light for those who cry aloud with faith: O supremely hymned God of our fathers, blessed art Thou!`,source:`Ode VII`,label:`Ode VII (feast), 3`},{text:`Hearkening to thy suffering entreaty, O Igor, Christ gave thee the strength and power to bear thy pangs. By thy supplications grant that I too may be steadfast amid the sufferings of life.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI (passion-bearer), 1`},{text:`Having strengthened thyself with prayer before the image of the most pure one, thou didst go forth to thine enemies who were seeking thee; and like an innocent lamb, suffered at their hands, and wast unjustly slain.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI (passion-bearer), 2`},{text:`Everyone who bringeth supplication unto thee with faith, O spiritual athlete, quickly receiveth the healing of the sufferings of their souls and bodies. Grant this also abundantly unto us, we pray.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI (passion-bearer), 3`},{text:`Accept thou our prayer, O most pure one, and with thine omnipotent supplication beseech God Who is the Lover of mankind and Who was born of thee, that He deliver us from enemies, visible and invisible, from sins and all torments.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI (passion-bearer), Theotokion`}]}],"09-20":{saint:`Afterfeast of the Exaltation of the Cross; Commemoration of the Holy Greatmartyr Eustathius & Those With Him`,oca_primary:!0,source_file:`09-20.pdf`,rank:`six_stichera`,fekula_section:`2G1`,fekula_section_override:`2G1`,has_great_doxology:!1,has_polyeleos:!1,has_litya:!1,has_paroemias:!1,magnificat_sung:!1,matins_format:`god_is_the_lord`,aposticha_source:`menaion`,it_is_truly_meet_suppressed:!0,note:"RANK PER encoding_rule_v2.md §1.1 WATERFALL, RUN IN FULL because ODS 3rd ed. vol. III does not cover 20 September (no `ods3-0920` entry), so §1.1 step 0 does not apply. Step 1 — not a Great Feast: this is the AFTERFEAST of the Exaltation; an afterfeast day is not itself on the closed enumerated list of Twelve. Step 2 — not Vigil: no Small Vespers in 09-20.pdf. Step 3 — not Polyeleos: no Polyeleos, no Magnification, no Matins Gospel. Step 4 — NOT DOXOLOGY, AND THIS IS THE STEP THAT ACTUALLY HAD TO BE TESTED HERE: 09-20.pdf DOES print Praises stichera ('On the Praises, 4 Stichera of the holy martyrs, by Andrew Pyrrhus') with a Glory of Germanus and a Both now of the feast, which superficially resembles doxology rank. But no Great Doxology is appointed, and the Praises are followed directly by 'On the Aposticha, these Stichera of the feast, in Tone II' — a Matins Aposticha, which exists only where the Great Doxology is NOT sung. Praises stichera at six-stichera rank are unusual but not disqualifying; the Matins Aposticha is decisive. Step 5 — Six-Stichera: 'On “Lord, I have cried ...,” 6 Stichera: 3 of the feast, in Tone IV ... And 3 Stichera of the holy great-martyr, in the same melody'. Feast-plus-saint split → Fekula §2G1, set as both fekula_section and fekula_section_override. NOTE ON 'IN THE SAME MELODY': the great-martyr's three stichera carry no tone or special melody of their own; the PDF says only 'in the same melody', so they inherit Tone IV / Spec. Mel. “Thou hast given a sign ...” from the feast group above them. That inheritance is recorded on each sticheron rather than left blank, and it is an inference from an explicit rubric, not a guess. APOSTICHA SOURCE IS MENAION, printed verbatim: 'On the Aposticha, these Stichera of the feast, in Tone II' (Spec. Mel. “O house of Ephratha ...”). Encoded with psalm verses on the stichera they precede. NOTE the printed punctuation difference between the two aposticha blocks in this file: the Vespers verse reads 'Exalt ye the Lord our God; and worship the footstool of His feet, * for He is holy' with a SEMICOLON, while the Matins verse uses a comma. The Vespers form is stored verbatim as printed. KONTAKION ROUTING (§5) — INVERTED RELATIVE TO 09-19, AND THIS IS NOT AN ERROR. On 19 September the martyrs' kontakion stands at Ode III and the Cross kontakion at Ode VI; on 20 September the SAME Cross kontakion ('O Thou Who wast lifted up willingly upon the Cross', Tone IV) is printed at ODE III and the Greatmartyr Eustathius' kontakion (Tone II, Spec. Mel. “Seeking the highest ...”) at ODE VI. Stored as kontakion_ode3 = feast/Cross → 1st & 6th Hours, kontakion_ode6 = Eustathius → 3rd & 9th Hours. The two consecutive dates therefore route the Cross kontakion to DIFFERENT Hours, which is a real property of the printed source and must not be normalized away. TWO IKOI, BOTH CAPTURED. The Cross ikos ('He who was caught up to the third heaven of paradise ...') is printed after the ODE III kontakion and is stored in `ikos_ode3`; Eustathius' ikos ('Grant me the gift of hymnody, O Lord my God ...') is printed after the ODE VI kontakion and is stored in `ikos`, keeping menaion-browser.jsx's hardcoded 'Ikos (after Ode VI)' label truthful. KNOWN DISPLAY GAP: menaion-browser.jsx does not render ikos_ode3, so the Cross ikos is in the data but not on screen. Note also that the 09-20 printing of the Cross ikos opens 'He who was caught up' while the 09-19 printing of the same ikos opens 'He that was caught up'; each is stored as printed in its own file rather than harmonized. GLORY AND BOTH NOW ARE SEPARATE HERE, UNLIKE 09-07 AND 09-19. At Lord I Have Cried the PDF prints 'Glory ..., of the martyrs, the composition of Ephraim of Karyes, in Tone II' and, separately, 'Both now ..., of the feast, the composition of Cyprian, in Tone II'. The Glory is in stichera_glory. The Both now is stored in `lic_theotokion`, which is the canonical Both-now carrier in this vocabulary (stichera_both_now was explicitly RETIRED in the project history in favor of lic_theotokion) — but the reader should know that the text is NOT a theotokion: it is a sticheron of the Cross about Jacob's crossed hands. Field name and content disagree; recorded rather than worked around. The Aposticha likewise has a genuine pair — 'Glory ..., of the holy great-martyr, by Ephraim of Karyes, in Tone VI' and 'Both now ..., of the feast, in Tone VI' — so aposticha_glory and aposticha_both_now are both populated with distinct texts. MULTIPLE LITURGY PROPERS — TWO PROKEIMENA, TWO ALLELUIA, TWO COMMUNION VERSES. The PDF prints 'Prokeimenon of the feast in Tone VII (also until the Leave-taking)' AND 'Prokeimenon of the saint, in Tone IV', with a single Verse ('I beheld the Lord ever before me ...') printed under the SAINT's prokeimenon. The vocabulary has prokeimenon_2_tone / prokeimenon_2_text but no prokeimenon_2_stichos, so the verse is stored in prokeimenon_stichos and prokeimenon_note states that it belongs to the second (saint's) prokeimenon, not the first. Alleluia: 'of the feast, in Tone IV' → alleluia_tone/alleluia_verse; 'of the saint, in Tone IV' with its own Verse → alleluia_2_tone / alleluia_2_verse / alleluia_2_stichos. Communion: the PDF prints one of the feast and one 'of the saints'; the vocabulary has a single communion_verse field, so both are stored in it with explicit 'Of the feast:' / 'Of the saints:' labels rather than one being dropped. menaion-browser.jsx does not currently render prokeimenon_2_* or alleluia_2_*. ZADOSTOINIK CAPTURED, as on 09-19: 'Instead of “It is truly meet ...,” we chant the Irmos of ODE IX of Canon I', refrain 'Magnify, O my soul, the all-honored Cross of the Lord!'. EXAPOSTILARION: the martyrs' exapostilarion (Spec. Mel. “By the Spirit in the sanctuary ...”) is marked (Twice) in the PDF and is stored in `exapostilarion`; the repeat is recorded in this note rather than by duplicating the text. The feast exapostilarion occupies the 'Glory ..., Both now ...' slot and is stored in matins_exapostilarion_theotokion — here the field name is APT, since that text is genuinely addressed to the Theotokos ('Placing our hope on thee, O Theotokos ...'). TROPARION ORDER: Vespers prints the martyrs' troparion first, then 'Glory ..., Both now ..., Troparion of the feast, in Tone I'; `troparion` = martyrs, `troparion_second` = feast. At 'God is the Lord' the order inverts (feast Twice, Glory martyrs, Both now feast). Note that the martyrs' troparion here is the PLURAL form of the same hymn used in the singular for Sozon on 09-07 ('In their sufferings, Thy martyrs O Lord ...' vs 'In his sufferings, Thy martyr Sozon O Lord ...'); both are stored as printed. BEATITUDES: 'On the Beatitudes, 8 Troparia; 4 from ODE VIII of the canon of the feast, and 4 from ODE VI of the canon of the holy martyrs.' The PDF prints 6 unique texts — 3 of the feast (first marked (Twice)) and 3 of the martyrs (first marked (Twice), the third being the Theotokion) — which fills the 8 slots. Encoded as 6 entries with (Twice) notes on the two repeated ones, beatitudes_count 8. OCA CHECK — NO OVERRIDE APPLIED. The PDF's commemoration matches the commemoration OCA lists as primary for 20 September (Greatmartyr Eustathius Placidas with his wife and sons). oca_primary is true on that basis. No live OCA lookup was possible in this pass (no network), so no hymn-by-hymn comparison was made and no §2.1 step 5 override was applied; the St. Sergius thou/thy text governs per §11 #17. ANOMALY WORTH FLAGGING TO THE EDITOR: the Canon rubric here reads 'Canon of the feast, with 6 Troparia, including the Irmos; And that of the holy martyrs, with 6 Troparia' — a 6+6 division, where 09-19 the day before prints 8+4 for the same afterfeast. The canons themselves are out of scope, but the differing division is a real datum about the two services' relative weighting and is recorded here. SCOPE: full canon odes (both canons), the two Sessional Hymns after the chantings of the Psalter, the Sessional Hymn of the great-martyr after Ode III (itself marked (Twice)), the Praises stichera with their Glory and Both now, and the Matins Aposticha are out of scope at this rank per §6 and §9 and are deliberately not captured.",feast_e:`Ephesians 6:10-17 (§233)`,feast_g:`Luke 21:12-19 (§106)`,prokeimenon_tone:7,prokeimenon_text:`Exalt ye the Lord our God, and worship the footstool of His feet; * for He is holy.`,prokeimenon_2_tone:4,prokeimenon_2_text:`In the saints that are in His earth hath the Lord been wondrous; * He hath wrought all His desires in them.`,prokeimenon_stichos:`I beheld the Lord ever before me, for He is at my right hand, that I might not be shaken.`,prokeimenon_note:`TWO prokeimena are printed: the first 'of the feast in Tone VII (also until the Leave-taking)', the second 'of the saint, in Tone IV'. The single printed Verse stands under the SAINT's prokeimenon, i.e. it belongs to prokeimenon_2_text; it is stored in prokeimenon_stichos only because the vocabulary has no prokeimenon_2_stichos field.`,alleluia_tone:4,alleluia_verse:`Remember Thy congregation which Thou hast purchased from the beginning.`,alleluia_2_tone:4,alleluia_2_verse:`The righteous cried, and the Lord heard them, and He delivered them out of all their tribulations.`,alleluia_2_stichos:`Many are the tribulations of the righteous, and the Lord shall deliver them out of them all.`,communion_verse:`Of the feast: The light of Thy countenance, O Lord, hath been signed upon us. Of the saints: Rejoice in the Lord, O ye righteous; praise is meet for the upright.`,stichera_lord_i_call_count:6,stichera_lord_i_call:[{tone:4,spec_mel:`Thou hast given a sign`,text:`Today Thy precious Cross * hath shone forth radiantly like the sun, O Savior Christ, * set up and elevated * on the most glorious place of the skull, * on Thy most holy mountain, * showing forth most manifestly * that it is thereby, O Omnipotent One, * that Thou hast raised our nature up to the heavens, ** in that Thou lovest mankind.`},{tone:4,spec_mel:`Thou hast given a sign`,text:`Today, O Unapproachable One, * the heavens have declared Thy glory unto mankind; * for the image of the Cross, * shining forth in radiance * with unapproachable light, * hath denounced the savage and cruel nature * of those who slew God. * Wherefore, we glorify Thy loving dispensation, ** O almighty Jesus, Savior of our souls.`},{tone:4,spec_mel:`Thou hast given a sign`,text:`By stretching forth his arms * Moses vanquished Amalek, * prefiguring the image of the Cross; * and we now, bowing down before the tree of the Cross, * trample underfoot the wiles and machinations of the enemy, * having Christ as our champion, * Who was lifted up upon it in the flesh, * slaying the serpent ** and saving mankind.`},{tone:4,spec_mel:`Thou hast given a sign`,text:`Thy calling was from on high, * and not from men, O Eustathius; * for Christ, the Lover of mankind, * Himself appeared to thee and ensnared thee, O blessed one, * in the nets of faith, * washing away thy sins * with thy baptism and the trials of life, * and, as Master, He extended patience to thee ** and showed thee to be a renowned victor.`},{tone:4,spec_mel:`Thou hast given a sign`,text:`Rejoicing, O Eustathius, * in thy youth thou didst receive instruction in the virtues, * and, bereft of thy children and thy spouse, * thou didst valiantly endure captivity, * suffering ignominious slavery. * But thy courage in battle * made thee loving * and rendered thee radiant, ** O ever-memorable one.`},{tone:4,spec_mel:`Thou hast given a sign`,text:`Thou didst offer thyself to God as divine incense * rendered most fragrant in the fire, * as a beautiful holocaust, * a right acceptable sacrifice, * and a living and most perfect offering, * with all thy household, most fruitful. * Wherefore, the mansions of heaven and the armies of the saints * have now received thee and thy family, ** O right wondrous Eustathius.`}],stichera_glory:{tone:2,text:`Eustathius hath placed himself before us as a second Job in life, in his sufferings and struggles, being a model of the virtues and a tower of patience, truly surpassing Job in that virtue, with his spouse and children, faithful in life and unshaken amid trials, a victor amid sufferings. Him do we set forth as a supplicant before Christ, that He grant unto our souls enlightenment and the cleansing of transgressions.`},lic_theotokion:{tone:2,text:`When the hands of the Patriarch Jacob were crossed at the blessing of Joseph’s children, the sign of Thy Cross was prefigured, and holding it as our steadfast protector, we mightily drive away the hordes of the demons and cast down the pride of Belial, in that which vanquished the pernicious power of the most malicious Amalek. With a right honorable mind, O ye pious faithful, let us reverently bear it forth, and uplift it now for the cleansing of our sins, and let us cry aloud with raised voices: Have mercy, O Lord! O good One Who wast incarnate of the Virgin, have pity on the wise creation of Thy hands!`},stichera_aposticha:[{tone:2,spec_mel:`O house of Ephratha`,text:`With Thy divinely created water * and Thy blood, O Word * is the Church * splendidly adorned like a bride, ** and it hymneth the glory of the Cross.`},{tone:2,spec_mel:`O house of Ephratha`,verse:`Exalt ye the Lord our God; and worship the footstool of His feet, * for He is holy.`,text:`Lifting high the spear and the Cross, * the nails and the other sacred things * wherewith the life-bearing body * of Christ was pierced, ** let us bow down before them.`},{tone:2,spec_mel:`O house of Ephratha`,verse:`God is our King before the ages; * He hath wrought salvation in the midst of the earth.`,text:`When Moses * lifted up his arms * in the form of the Cross, * and vanquished Amalek, * he portrayed the most pure suffering of Christ.`}],aposticha_glory:{tone:6,text:`O thou who art adamantly firm in soul, how can we praise thee as is meet? For, deprived of thy spouse, thou didst transcend nature, possessions and children, and didst utter the blessed and ever-memorable cry of Job: “The Lord hath given, and the Lord hath taken away! As the Lord hath willed, so hath it been!” But God whom thou didst love and Whom thou didst fervently desire again gave thee most cherished things, O thou who took thought beforehand concerning those who suffered with thee. And having reached the end of divers torments with them, and receiving them as thy fellow supplicants, O blessed Eustathius, patient of soul, pray thou, that we be delivered from our iniquities.`},aposticha_both_now:{tone:6,text:`O Cross of Christ, hope of Christians, guide of the lost, haven of the tempest-tossed, victory amid battles, confirmation of the whole world, physician of the ill, resurrection of the dead: Have mercy upon us!`},troparion:{tone:4,text:`In their sufferings, Thy martyrs O Lord, * received imperishable crowns from Thee, our God; * for, possessed of Thy might, * they set at naught the tyrants and crushed the feeble audacity of the demons. ** By their supplications save Thou our souls.`},troparion_second:{tone:1,text:`Save O Lord Thy people, * and bless Thine inheritance. * Grant now unto the faithful * victory over adversaries, * and by the power of Thy Cross ** do Thou preserve Thy commonwealth.`},kontakion_ode3:{tone:4,text:`O Thou Who wast lifted up willingly upon the Cross, * bestow Thy mercies upon the new community named after Thee, * O Christ God; gladden with Thy power Orthodox Christians, * granting them victory over enemies; ** may they have as Thy help the invincible trophy, the weapon of peace.`},kontakion_ode6:{tone:2,spec_mel:`Seeking the highest`,text:`Manifestly emulating the sufferings of Christ, * and having drained His cup with fervor, * O Eustathius, thou wast a partaker and fellow heir of glory, * receiving divine remission of sins from on high ** from the God of all Himself.`},ikos_ode3:`He who was caught up to the third heaven of paradise and heard unspeakable and divine words which the human tongue cannot utter, what writeth he to the Galatians, which, as lovers of the Scriptures, ye have both read and come to understand? God forbid, saith he, that I should glory, save only in the Cross of the Lord, whereon having suffered He slew the passions. Let us all then firmly hold this boast, the Cross of the Lord; for this Wood is our salvation, the invincible trophy, the weapon of peace.`,ikos:`Grant me the gift of hymnody, O Lord my God, that I may now hymn and speak of the struggles of Thy spiritual athlete, that in good order I may praise Eustathius, who was valiant in sufferings, who was ever the victor in battle against the enemy, who was great in piety, and who hath shone forth among the choir of the martyrs. For together with them and the angels, he chanteth unceasingly to thee, O all-wise One, receiving divine remission from on high.`,exapostilarion:`With thy divinely wise children and Thine all-blessed wife thou hast received the glory of immortality and never-ending life, O blessed Eustathius, having abandoned the pleasures of the world. Wherefore, with love we celebrate thy most sacred and divine memory.`,matins_exapostilarion_theotokion:{text:`Placing our hope on thee, O Theotokos, we win a most perfect victory over twofold foes, having the Cross of thy Son as our weapon and trophy, our shield, sword, spear and arrow against Belial.`},beatitudes_source:`menaion`,beatitudes_count:8,beatitudes_troparia:[{text:`O hosts of heaven, chant ye to the exalted Tree which was drenched in the blood of the incarnate Word of God, and celebrate the restoration of those born on earth. O Ye people, worship the Cross of Christ, by which the world hath been granted the resurrection throughout all ages!`,source:`Ode VIII`,label:`Ode VIII, 1`,note:`(Twice) per PDF`},{text:`O ye mortal stewards of grace, with your hands reverently raise on high the Cross whereon Christ God stood and the spear which pierced the body of God the Word, that all the nations may see the salvation which is of God, glorifying Him throughout all ages!`,source:`Ode VIII`,label:`Ode VIII, 2`},{text:`O faithful Christian kings, forechosen by divine decree, be ye glad! And having received from God the precious Cross, rejoice in it, the weapon of victory, for thereby tribes of enemy warriors seeking to make battle have been scattered throughout all ages.`,source:`Ode VIII`,label:`Ode VIII, 3`},{text:`Thou didst desire Christ Who had appeared to Thee, O glorious one, and didst unite thyself to Him with love; for thou wast shown to be adorned, clad in the purple robe of thy blood.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI, 1`,note:`(Twice) per PDF`},{text:`Thou didst make haste to follow the divine steps of Christ, and, crowned with all thy household, O martyr, wast most perfectly shown to share in His sufferings and kingdom.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI, 2`},{text:`Using now the words of Gabriel, O all-immaculate Virgin, we cry out: Rejoice, O only blessed Mother! Rejoice, O thou who didst have within thee the Sun of righteousness!`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI, Theotokion`}],instead_of_it_is_truly_meet_refrain:`Magnify, O my soul, the all-honored Cross of the Lord!`,instead_of_it_is_truly_meet_irmos:`O Theotokos, thou art a mystical Paradise, * who untilled brought forth Christ. * by Whom the life-giving Tree of the Cross hath been planted upon the earth: * Therefore celebrating its exaltation on this day, * Him do we worship * and thee do we magnify.`},"09-26":{saint:`Commemoration of the Repose of the Holy Apostle & Evangelist John the Theologian`,oca_primary:!0,source_file:`09-26.pdf`,rank:`vigil`,fekula_section:`2F`,has_great_doxology:!0,has_polyeleos:!0,has_litya:!0,has_paroemias:!0,magnificat_sung:!0,matins_format:`god_is_the_lord`,matins_gospel:`John 21:15-23 (§67)`,aposticha_source:`menaion`,magnification:`We magnify thee, O apostle of Christ and evangelist John the theologian, and we honor thy pangs and labors, wherewith thou didst struggle in the proclamation of the Gospel of Christ.`,magnification_selected_psalm:`The heavens declare the glory of God, and the firmament proclaimeth the work of His hands.`,note:"RANK PER encoding_rule_v2.md §1.1 WATERFALL, RUN IN FULL. ODS 3rd ed. vol. III does NOT cover 26 September (there is no `ods3-0926` entry among the 77), so §1.1 step 0 does not apply and no printed rank exists to short-circuit the test. Step 1 (Great Feast) is negative — the Repose of the Theologian is not on step 1's closed enumerated list of Great Feasts of the Master or of the Theotokos. Step 2 (Vigil) is POSITIVE and decisive: 09-26.pdf prints a dedicated 'AT LITTLE VESPERS' section on p.1, which is step 2's stated diagnostic ('the Menaion PDF provides a Small Vespers for this saint'). The test stops there; steps 3-6 were not reached. Corroborating evidence, none of it diagnostic on its own: 'Blessed is the man ...,' the first Antiphon at Great Vespers; a Litiya with the Blessing of the Loaves; the Polyeleos with Magnification; the Great Doxology; the PDF's own conditional 'But if a vigil is not served, then after the Troparion:'; and the closing anointing rubric, which applies 'whenever there are feasts of major saints whose services merit the holding of vigil'. LITTLE VESPERS EXISTS BUT IS NOT ENCODED. It is the rank diagnostic above and is recorded here so that its omission reads as a scope decision rather than a gap: V1 has no field vocabulary for Little Vespers (§6). The PDF prints, on pp. 1-2, 4 stichera of the evangelist at Lord I Have Cried in Tone II (spec. mel. 'When from the Tree ...'), a Glory and a Theotokion, Aposticha stichera in Tone I, and the troparion in Tone II with its Tone II theotokion. None of it is captured below. KONTAKION ROUTING (§5): a SINGLE kontakion, of the holy evangelist, Tone II, printed after Ode VI with its Ikos and reprinted verbatim AT LITURGY. Stored as kontakion_ode6 + ikos; per §5 the assembler routes a single Menaion kontakion to all four Hours, and the PDF's own Hours rubric agrees ('At the First Hour, the Troparion and Kontakion of the saint are read'). All three §5.x positions were checked — AT LITURGY, Ode VI and Ode III — and no second kontakion is printed anywhere; kontakion_ode3 is genuinely ABSENT, not unlocated. LITIYA — DEDICATED MENAION STICHERA ARE PRINTED (§12 vigil rule). PDF p.4: 'At Litiya, the Sticheron of the temple, and these Stichera of the apostle, in Tone I', followed by FOUR proper stichera (the composition of Germanus; the composition of Andrew Pyrrhus; and two unattributed), then 'Glory ...' in Tone IV and 'Both now ..., Theotokion, in Tone IV'. All six texts are encoded in litya_stichera / litya_glory / litya_both_now. This is NOT the 05-27A case: the temple sticheron is named in addition to, not instead of, the Menaion stichera, and it is parish-variable and not a Menaion text, so it is not encoded. PAROEMIA ANOMALY — THE THREE LESSONS ARE NEW TESTAMENT, NOT OLD. The PDF prints 'Entrance. Prokeimenon of the day. Three Lessons:' and then three readings from the General Epistle of John (1 John 3:21-4:6; 4:11-16; 4:20-5:5). There are THREE, not four. This is the traditional appointment for the Theologian — his own epistle read in the paroemia slot in place of Old Testament lessons — and it is recorded as an anomaly relative to the usual expectation, not corrected. The paroemia_1/2/3 fields are retained because these readings occupy the paroemia slot at the Entrance; the field name's Old-Testament connotation is the mismatch, flagged here rather than worked around with an invented field. The PDF DOES print full chapter-and-verse citations in these headings, so no identification was needed. LIC: 8 slots from 3 unique texts, Tone I, spec. mel. 'Joy of the ranks of heaven ...' — printed (Thrice), (Thrice), (Twice). 8 is not an integer multiple of 3, so uniform doubling cannot express it and positional repeatIndex markers are used per §6b, at array indices 1, 2, 4, 5 and 7. OCA CHECK — no override applied, no divergence. OCA's 26 September primary commemoration is 'Repose of the Holy Apostle and Evangelist John the Theologian' — same date, same commemoration, so no §1 date or primacy adjustment. OCA's Troparion Tone 2 and Kontakion Tone 2 are the same two hymns the PDF prints, in contemporary register; per §11 #17 the thou/thy PDF text governs. OCA lists no second troparion or kontakion for the evangelist, so §2.1 step 5 (OCA override where the daily PDF lacks a proper) does not arise. OCA additionally commemorates Ven. Ephraim of Perekop and the Arrival of the Ivḗron Icon in Georgia on this date; neither has any proper in 09-26.pdf and neither is encoded. MAGNIFICAT: magnificat_sung is set true as the DEFAULT, not from a printed rubric. The PDF prints the Ode IX irmoi but no 'More honorable' / Magnificat rubric; nothing on this date displaces the Magnificat, so it is sung. Recorded as an inference, not as a source claim. MATINS APOSTICHA: ABSENT by rubric, not unencoded — the four Praises stichera with their Glory and Both now are followed directly by the Great Doxology (PDF p.21), which displaces the Matins Aposticha. EXTRACTION ARTIFACTS, NOT SOURCE DEFECTS. (1) The Liturgy scripture headings extract as 'A READING FROM THE GENERAL EPISTLE OF JOHN, §73, (1 IN. 4: 12-19)' and 'GOSPEL ACCORDING TO JOHN, §61 (IN. 19: 25-27, 21: 24-25)' — 'IN.' is an OCR misreading of the printed 'JN.', corrected to John in feast_e / feast_g. (2) Two line-break hyphens were rejoined: 're-' + 'vealing' in the Vespers doxasticon and 'Where-' + 'fore' in the second Aposticha sticheron. Both are extraction artifacts of justified type, distinct from the §1.3 errata class, which are defects in the printed book itself. 'there-from' (twice) and the mid-line compounds are printed that way and are kept verbatim. TWO APPARENT TYPOGRAPHICAL DEFECTS IN THE PRINTED SOURCE, KEPT VERBATIM AND FLAGGED rather than silently corrected: the Vespers doxasticon reads 'the sun of thunder' where the Praises doxasticon and the second exapostilarion both read 'son of thunder'; and the Litiya 'Both now' reads 'having raising it on high to the heavens'. Recorded, not fixed — correcting a printed source is out of scope for encoding. LITURGY RUBRIC recorded, not encoded: the PDF's post-Entrance rubric makes the troparion and kontakion order conditional on the dedication of the temple (to the Lord, the Theotokos, a saint, or none), which is parish-variable. The saint's own troparion and kontakion, printed immediately after that rubric, are what is stored. SCOPE: three full canons (of the Theotokos and two of the evangelist), the sessional hymns before and after the Polyeleos, and the four Praises stichera with their Glory and Both now are all present in the PDF and are deliberately NOT captured. §6 puts them out of scope at every rank below Great Feast, Vigil rank included.",feast_e:`First John 4:12-19 (§73)`,feast_g:`John 19:25-27, 21:24-25 (§61)`,prokeimenon_tone:8,prokeimenon_text:`Their sound hath gone forth into all the earth, * and their words unto the ends of the world.`,prokeimenon_stichos:`The heavens declare the glory of God, and the firmament proclaimeth the work of His hands.`,alleluia_tone:1,alleluia_verse:`The heavens shall confess Thy wonders, O Lord, and Thy truth in the congregation of saints.`,alleluia_stichos:`God Who is glorified in the council of the saints.`,communion_verse:`Their sound hath gone forth into all the earth, and their words unto the ends of the world.`,matins_prokeimenon:`Their sound hath gone forth into all the earth, * and their words unto the ends of the world. (Tone IV) Verse: The heavens declare the glory of God, and the firmament proclaimeth the work of His hands.`,paroemia_1:`First Epistle of John — “if our heart condemn us not, then have we confidence toward God”; try the spirits (First John 3:21-4:6)`,paroemia_2:`First Epistle of John — “God is love; and he that dwelleth in love dwelleth in God” (First John 4:11-16)`,paroemia_3:`First Epistle of John — he that loveth God must love his brother also; faith overcometh the world (First John 4:20-5:5)`,stichera_lord_i_call_count:8,stichera_lord_i_call:[{tone:1,spec_mel:`Joy of the ranks of heaven`,text:`The beholder of ineffable revelations * and extoller of the highest mysteries of God, * the son of Zebedee, * who set down in writing the Gospel of Christ, * hath taught us to theologize ** concerning the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit.`},{repeatIndex:0},{repeatIndex:0},{tone:1,spec_mel:`Joy of the ranks of heaven`,text:`The harp of heavenly hymns played by God, * the recorder of mysteries, the divinely eloquent mouth, * doth beautifully chant the hymn of hymns; * for, moving his lips as though they were strings, * and using his tongue as a plectrum, ** he prayeth that we be saved.`},{repeatIndex:3},{repeatIndex:3},{tone:1,spec_mel:`Joy of the ranks of heaven`,text:`Proclaiming with thy thunderous tongue * the hidden words of divine wisdom, O beloved of God, * thou ever criest aloud, continually moving thy lips: * In the beginning was the Word! * And thereby instructest every man ** in the knowledge of God.`},{repeatIndex:6}],stichera_glory:{tone:2,text:`O ye of the race of mortals, let us offer goodly praise as is meet to the beloved and virginal John, the sun of thunder, the foundation of the words of God, the author of theology, the first preacher of the truth of the dogmas of the wisdom of God; for having the divine continually within him, he said: In the beginning was the Word, Who is inseparable from the Father and of the same essence with the Father, revealing to us through himself the Orthodoxy of the Holy Trinity. And he hath likewise shown us that He createth with the Father and beareth life and the light of truth. O awesome wonder! O uttermost wisdom! For, full of love, he was also filled with theology through glory, honor and faith, as a founder of our pure Faith. Wherefore, we shall receive everlasting blessings on the day of judgment.`},lic_theotokion:{tone:2,text:`The shadow of the law hath passed now that grace hath come, * for as the bush wrapped in flame was not consumed, * so didst thou bear a Child O Virgin * and remained a virgin; * in place of a pillar of fire, the Sun of righteousness hath dawned, * instead of Moses, Christ is come, ** the salvation of our souls.`},litya_stichera:[{tone:1,text:`The composition of Germanus: Rivers of theology poured forth from thine honored mouth, O apostle, and the Church of God, watered thereby, doth worship the consubstantial Trinity in Orthodoxy. Pray now to the Trinity, O theologian John, that our souls be made steadfast and saved.`},{tone:1,text:`The composition of Andrew Pyrrhus: The garden of purity hath emitted for us the myrrh of sweet fragrance on this present feast, that we may cry out to him: O apostle John, who reclined against the Master’s breast, who hast rained down discourse upon the world and preserved the Virgin as the apple of thine eye, beseech Christ, that He grant us great mercy.`},{tone:1,text:`O disciple of the Savior, virgin and theologian, when Christ God was crucified He committed the Virgin Theotokos to thy care, in that thou art virginal; and thou didst preserve her as the apple of thine eye. Wherefore, pray thou, that our souls be saved.`},{tone:1,text:`As an eyewitness to ineffable mysteries, thou didst cry out, exclaiming: “In the beginning the pre-eternal Word was with God, and He was God!”, O apostle John, intimate and faithful friend of Christ, sweetness of the Trinity, unshakable confirmation of Ephesus and Patmos, our help. Pray thou, O all-blessed theologian, that the people who ever celebrate thy memory with faith be delivered from wicked enemies, material and noetic.`}],litya_glory:{tone:4,text:`Reclining against the breast of Christ the Teacher at the Lord’s supper, O beloved disciple, thou didst thereby come to know ineffable things, and hast thundered forth thy heavenly voice unto all, saying; “In the beginning was the Word and the Word was with God, and the Word was God” - Christ God, the Savior of our souls the Light of truth Who enlighteneth every man who cometh into the world.`},litya_both_now:{tone:4,text:`The Son who together with the Father and the Spirit * is glorified in the highest by the seraphim, * wishing to refashion the First-formed man, * ineffably emptied His entire being into thy womb, * O all-praised Theotokos. * Dawning forth from thee * He hath enlightened the whole world by His Godhead, * delivering it from the deception of idolatry, * and by this He hath rendered the race of mankind divine, * having raising it on high to the heavens; ** Christ God the Savior of our souls.`},stichera_aposticha:[{tone:4,spec_mel:`Called from on high`,text:`Theologizing concerning the Son of the Most High * Who with the Father is equally everlasting and of the same essence, * immutable Light from Light, * the impress of the hypostasis of the Father, * Who shone forth timelessly and dispassionately from Him, * the Creator and Lord of all the ages, * Christ our God, * didst thou preach to the world, O beloved one, * as He Who brought light forth out of darkness. * Him do thou entreat, ** that He save and enlighten our souls.`},{tone:4,spec_mel:`Called from on high`,verse:`Their sound hath gone forth into all the earth, * and their words unto the ends of the world.`,text:`Receiving the light of the Comforter, * and, illumined therewith, thou didst theologize * and proclaim unto all, O beloved one, * that He proceedeth from the Father * and through the Son is revealed to mankind, * equal in honor, co-enthroned and of the same essence * with the beginningless Father and God the Word. * Wherefore, we honor thee in hymns * as the foundation of the divine Faith, * which do thou preserve unshaken ** through thy supplications to the Lord.`},{tone:4,spec_mel:`Called from on high`,verse:`The heavens declare the glory of God, * and the firmament proclaimeth the work of His hands.`,text:`Having ascended to the summit of theology, * thou didst learn the ineffable mysteries of God: * the single essence of the Godhead, * His single glory, * kingdom and dominion, * ever distinct in three Hypostases, * yet essentially indivisible and united in an uncommingled divine unity. * And, rendering glory, O theologian, * thou didst preach the indivisible Trinity. * Him do thou entreat, ** that He save and enlighten our souls.`}],aposticha_glory:{tone:6,text:`O apostle of Christ, evangelist and theologian, as an initiate of ineffable mysteries thou hast thundered forth upon us the ineffable doctrines of wisdom, explaining to the faithful that He was in the beginning, and discounting that there was a time when He did not exist, thus rejecting the notions of the heretics. And as thou wast shown to be the beloved intimate and friend of Christ, like the eloquent Isaiah and Moses the God-seer, pray thou earnestly for our souls, in that thou hast boldness before God.`},aposticha_both_now:{tone:6,text:`Christ the Lord, my Creator and Redeemer, * Who came forth from thy womb, O most pure one, * and clothed Himself in my nature, * hath freed Adam from the primal curse. * Wherefore, like the angel * we unceasingly cry out to thee, * O all-pure one, * who art truly the Mother of God and Virgin: * Rejoice!, O Sovereign Lady, ** the intercession, protection and salvation of our souls!`},troparion:{tone:2,text:`O beloved apostle of Christ God, * hasten thou to deliver a defenseless people. * He Who permitted thee to recline against His breast receiveth thee, * prostrate in supplication. * Him do thou beseech, O theologian, * that He dispel the gloom of the nations which doth beset us, ** asking for us peace and great mercy.`},kontakion_ode6:{tone:2,text:`Who can recount thy mighty works, O virgin apostle? * For thou pourest out miracles, causing healings to flow forth; * and thou prayest for our souls, * in that thou art a theologian ** and the friend of Christ.`},ikos:`It is a bold and unattainable thing to study the heights of heaven and to plumb the depths of the sea; for as it is impossible to calculate the number of the stars and the sands of the shore, so is it impossible to speak sufficiently of the theologian, whom Christ loved and crowned with so many crowns. For, reclining against His breast, he ate with Him at the mystical supper, in that he is a theologian and friend of Christ.`,gospel_sticheron:{tone:2,text:`O virgin theologian, beloved disciple of the Savior, by thy supplications save us from all harm, we pray, for we are thy flock.`},matins_exapostilarion_feast:{spec_mel:`By the Spirit in the sanctuary`,text:`O blessed virgin theologian, the Word chose thee as one virginal in soul and body, and showed thee to be a wondrous recorder and servant of His divinity; and when thou didst pass away from the earth, thou didst not die but dost ever live in God, remaining immortal.`},exapostilarion:`As a son of thunder thou didst proclaim theology unto all, saying: “In the beginning was the Word”, O apostle John; for, reclining against the breast of thy Master with faith and drawing forth there-from streams of theology, thou dost water all creation.`,matins_exapostilarion_theotokion:{text:`Standing before the Cross of thy Son and God with the virginal disciple, the all-pure one heard the Creator say: “Behold thy son!” And He said to the disciple: “Behold thy Mother!” With him we all hymn thee, O Virgin Birthgiver of God.`},beatitudes_source:`menaion`,beatitudes_troparia:[{text:`With the tongue of thy theology thou didst reveal the mystery of the Trinity which passeth understanding, O divinely blessed John; and therein hath my heart been established.`,source:`Ode III`,label:`Ode III, 1`,note:`(Twice) per PDF`},{text:`Thy tongue became the scribe’s pen of the all-holy Spirit, recording thy precious and divine Gospel with godly script.`,source:`Ode III`,label:`Ode III, 2`},{text:`Reclining against the Well-spring of wisdom with divinely wise boldness, O all-wise one, thou didst draw forth an abyss of wisdom; and became His godly herald.`,source:`Ode III`,label:`Ode III, 3`},{text:`Thy tongue of theology was truly like a writer’s pen, inscribing on the tablets of our hearts true understanding and the truly new law, O theologian.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI, 1`},{text:`O glorious one who, like a most excellent husbandman, caused the offshoots of impiety to wither at the root, plant thou the fear of God in my soul, that it may blossom forth with the fruitfulness of the virtues.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI, 2`},{text:`He Who most manifestly issued forth from the Virgin, called thee her son. With her make thou entreaty, that those who do that which is well-pleasing unto Him become children of God by adoption, O ever all-memorable one.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI, 3`},{text:`Through thee, O all-immaculate one, God appeared to mankind in the flesh. Him do thou ever entreat, that He slay the pernicious wisdom of our flesh, O all-pure one.`,source:`Ode VI`,label:`Ode VI, Theotokion`}]},"09-27":{saint:`Holy Martyr Callistratus & His Companions`,oca_primary:!0,source_file:`09-27.pdf`,rank:`simple`,fekula_section:`2A`,has_great_doxology:!1,has_polyeleos:!1,has_litya:!1,has_paroemias:!1,magnificat_sung:!0,matins_format:`god_is_the_lord`,aposticha_source:`octoechos`,note:"Rank per encoding_rule_v2.md §1.1 waterfall, RUN IN FULL because ODS 3rd ed. vol. III does NOT cover 27 September (no `ods3-0927` entry among the 77), so §1.1 step 0 does not apply. Step 1 Great Feast — NO: not on the closed enumerated list. Step 2 Vigil — NO: 09-27.pdf prints no Small Vespers; its Vespers heading is the plain 'AT VESPERS', not even 'AT GREAT VESPERS'. Step 3 Polyeleos — NO: Matins appoints no Polyeleos, no Magnification and no Selected Psalm; the Matins rubric is the ordinary weekday one, 'Both canons from the Oktoechos, with 8 Troparia; and that of the martyrs, with 4 Troparia, the composition of Joseph, in Tone VIII'. Step 4 Doxology — NO: no Great Doxology is appointed anywhere; the PDF prints no Praises stichera and no Matins dismissal rubric. Step 5 Six-Stichera — NO: the PDF appoints 'On \"Lord, I have cried ...,\" 3 Stichera of the holy martyrs, in Tone VIII', three and not six. Step 6 Simple — YES: 3 stichera at Lord I Have Cried for a SINGLE commemoration. Callistratus, Gymnasius and those who suffered with them are one martyric company under one heading, so §1.1's alternative Simple-rank form (6 stichera split 3+3 for a double of two saints) does not apply — Gymnasius is named inside the third sticheron and throughout the canon, but he is not given a separate service. KONTAKION ROUTING — DIVERGES FROM THE SINGLE-KONTAKION ASSUMPTION. The PDF prints TWO DISTINCT kontakia, not one text printed twice: after ODE III (p.3), 'Kontakion of the holy martyrs, in Tone IV', Spec. Mel. 'Thou hast appeared' — 'The Church, making every effort today, * doth offer up mystic praise for those who suffered for her, ** O holy and divine martyrs, right victorious and all-wise.'; and after ODE VI (p.4), 'Kontakion of the holy martyrs, in Tone IV', Spec. Mel. 'Having been lifted up on the Cross' — 'Having endured multifarious wounds * and manifestly received crowns from God...'. They share a tone (IV) and a heading wording but they are different hymns with different special melodies and different texts. The AT LITURGY section (p.6) confirms this by printing BOTH under explicit position labels: 'ODE III, Kontakion of the holy martyrs, in Tone IV' and 'ODE VI, Kontakion of the holy martyrs, in Tone IV'. They are therefore encoded as `kontakion_ode3` and `kontakion_ode6` per §5 routing — Ode III to the 1st and 6th Hours, Ode VI to the 3rd and 9th — and NOT collapsed into a single `kontakion_ode6`. The Ikos ('Gazing upon the shrine of thy relics, O Callistratus...') is printed immediately after the Ode VI kontakion and pairs with it, so it is stored in `ikos`, which menaion-browser.jsx labels 'Ikos (after Ode VI)' — truthful here. LORD I HAVE CRIED: 3 slots from 3 unique texts, all Tone VIII, Spec. Mel. 'O most glorious wonder', all pointed with * and **. Nothing is marked '(Twice)', so no repeat markers (§6b). Per §2A the other three stichera of the six sung come from the Octoechos and are not Menaion texts, so they are absent here by rubric, not unencoded. BOTH NOW IS A STAVROTHEOTOKION, AND THE ALTERNATIVE THEOTOKION IS NOT PRINTED. The PDF reads 'Both now ..., Theotokion, or this Stavrotheotokion, in Tone IV' and then prints ONLY the Stavrotheotokion ('Upon beholding Thee, * the Lamb and Shepherd, upon the Tree...'), Spec. Mel. 'As one valiant among the martyrs'. It is stored in `lic_stavrotheotokion`. `lic_theotokion` is ABSENT because the alternative theotokion is not printed in the source — it is taken from the Octoechos appendix on non-Wed/Fri days. In 2026, 27 September falls on a Sunday, so neither the Stavrotheotokion nor a weekday Menaion theotokion would be used as printed; per §1.1's governing principle the rank and content recorded here are the saint's own as printed in the Menaion, and Sunday layering is the assembler's job. NO VESPERS APOSTICHA IN THE PDF. The source goes straight from the Both-now Stavrotheotokion to the Troparion, printing no Aposticha section at all — the Aposticha is from the Octoechos at §2A. `stichera_aposticha` and `aposticha_glory` are therefore absent by rubric, and `aposticha_glory_absent: true` declares that explicitly so the validator's Check G sees an intentional absence rather than a gap. NOTHING AT LITURGY EXCEPT TROPARION AND KONTAKIA. The PDF's 'AT LITURGY' section (p.6) prints ONLY the troparion and the two kontakia. It prints NO Beatitudes rubric, NO Beatitudes troparia, NO prokeimenon, NO Epistle, NO Alleluia, NO Gospel and NO communion verse. `feast_e`, `feast_g`, `prokeimenon_*`, `alleluia_*`, `communion_verse`, `beatitudes_source` and `beatitudes_troparia` are therefore ABSENT from this entry — absent from the printed source, not omitted in encoding. At §2A the Liturgy propers come from the day of the week and the Octoechos unless the saint has his own, which is the ordinary simple-rank pattern; the entry records the absence rather than importing propers from another book. NO MATINS GOSPEL, PROKEIMENON OR MAGNIFICATION — none is appointed at simple rank and none is printed, so `matins_gospel`, `matins_prokeimenon`, `magnification` and `magnification_selected_psalm` are absent by rubric. MATINS FORMAT: the PDF prints no opening Matins rubric at all (no 'God is the Lord', no 'Alleluia'). `matins_format` is set to god_is_the_lord because 27 September is an ordinary non-fasting day with a commemorated saint, which is the 'God is the Lord' case; this is inferred from the calendar position, not read off the page, and is flagged here as such. `magnificat_sung` is true for the same reason: nothing in the PDF suppresses the Magnificat and 27 September lies outside every fore- and afterfeast (the Exaltation's leave-taking is 21 September). OCA CHECK: OCA lists the Martyr Callistratus and his 49 companions on 27 September as a primary commemoration, so `oca_primary` is true and no date divergence arises. SCOPE: the full canon (Odes I, III-IX with their irmoi and theotokia) and the sessional hymns with their theotokion and stavrotheotokion are printed in the PDF and are deliberately NOT captured, per §6 and the encoding scope for ranks below Great Feast.",stichera_lord_i_call_count:3,stichera_lord_i_call:[{tone:8,spec_mel:`O most glorious wonder`,text:`O martyred spiritual athlete Callistratus, * with thy mind illumined * by the light of piety, * thou didst unwaveringly tread the path * which leadeth to heavenly blessedness, * having vanquished all the wiles of the adversary. * Wherefore, we praise thee, * honoring thy sacred memory, ** O most blessed one.`},{tone:8,spec_mel:`O most glorious wonder`,text:`O divinely wise martyr Callistratus, * enriched by the words of life, * thou didst guide unto life * those who before were dead in accursedness through ignorance; * and, thou didst die with great zeal, O glorious one, * for they who believe in Christ, Who is the resurrection of us all, * most truly dwell in Him. * With them remember us ** to the supremely good Lord.`},{tone:8,spec_mel:`O most glorious wonder`,text:`O sacred martyr Gymnasius, * beheaded by the sword, * thou didst truly drown the Pharaoh of delusion * in the torrent of thy blood; * and now thou pourest forth healings upon all * who have recourse to thy temple with faith, * and therein praise thy struggles, * and celebrate thine honored memory, ** O blessed one.`}],stichera_glory:{tone:4,text:`Before Thy precious Cross, death was terrifying to mortals; but after Thy glorious Passion, mortals were terrifying to death. Thus the spiritual athlete was able to abolish all the power of the enemy: Through his supplications save our souls, O Christ.`},lic_stavrotheotokion:{tone:4,spec_mel:`As one valiant among the martyrs`,text:`Upon beholding Thee, * the Lamb and Shepherd, upon the Tree, * the ewe-lamb who bore Thee lamented, * and maternally exclaimed to Thee: * “O most desired Son, * how is it that Thou art suspended upon the tree of the Cross? * How is it that Thine arms and legs are nailed * by the iniquitous ones, O long-suffering Word, ** and that Thou hast shed Thy blood, O Master?”`},aposticha_glory_absent:!0,troparion:{tone:4,text:`In their sufferings, Thy martyrs O Lord, * received imperishable crowns from Thee, our God; * for, possessed of Thy might, * they set at naught the tyrants and crushed the feeble audacity of the demons. ** By their supplications save Thou our souls.`},kontakion_ode3:{tone:4,spec_mel:`Thou hast appeared`,text:`The Church, making every effort today, * doth offer up mystic praise for those who suffered for her, ** O holy and divine martyrs, right victorious and all-wise.`},kontakion_ode6:{tone:4,spec_mel:`Having been lifted up on the Cross`,text:`Having endured multifarious wounds * and manifestly received crowns from God, * pray to Christ for us who celebrate your most festive memorial, * O great Callistratus * and ye blessed ones who suffered with him, * that He may grant peace to His flock and people; ** for He is the confirmation of the faithful.`},ikos:`Gazing upon the shrine of thy relics, O Callistratus, I hymn and magnify the pangs and sufferings which thou didst suffer. Wherefore, I beseech thee: Render Christ, the Judge of thy contest, merciful to me, that by grace He grant me utterance with divine words, that I may be able to praise thy struggles; for no man is able to recount thy mighty deeds; how thou didst manfully enter the tribunal, denouncing the wicked thoughts of the tyrants. Christ is the confirmation of the faithful!`,exapostilarion:`O the wonder! How have those who are clay by nature been shown to be golden? For neither fire, nor the sword, nor the teeth of wild beasts, nor tortures, nor wounds destroyed them; and, having cast down the incorporeal one with their flesh, they pray for us to the Lord.`,matins_exapostilarion_theotokion:{text:`Rejoice, O palace of God! Rejoice, O mountain densely wooded! Rejoice, O divine table! Rejoice, bush unburnt! Rejoice, throne of glory! Rejoice, golden jar! Rejoice, most radiant lamp! Rejoice, O Mary, Virgin Mother, thou light cloud!`}}};export{e as default};